WEBVTT

1
00:01:19.239 --> 00:01:21.730
You did a wonderful job.
Wonderful.

2
00:01:28.749 --> 00:01:32.241
To continue, you've listened to
a long and complex case...

3
00:01:32.319 --> 00:01:34.253
murder in the first degree.

4
00:01:34.321 --> 00:01:39.315
Premeditated murder is the most serious
charge tried in our criminal courts.

5
00:01:39.393 --> 00:01:42.123
You've listened to the testimony.
You've had the law read to you...

6
00:01:42.196 --> 00:01:44.130
and interpreted
as it applies in this case.

7
00:01:44.198 --> 00:01:48.931
It's now your duty to sit down and try
and separate the facts from the fancy.

8
00:01:49.002 --> 00:01:52.438
One man is dead.
Another man's life is at stake.

9
00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.367
If there's a reasonable doubt in your
mind as to the guilt of the accused...

10
00:01:57.444 --> 00:02:02.279
a reasonable doubt... then you must
bring me a verdict of not guilty.

11
00:02:02.349 --> 00:02:06.445
If however there's no reasonable doubt,
then you must in good conscience...

12
00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:09.421
find the accused guilty.

13
00:02:09.489 --> 00:02:13.425
However you decide,
your verdict must be unanimous.

14
00:02:13.493 --> 00:02:16.951
In the event that you find
the accused guilty...

15
00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:20.363
the bench will not entertain
a recommendation for mercy.

16
00:02:20.434 --> 00:02:23.528
The death sentence is mandatory
in this case.

17
00:02:23.604 --> 00:02:27.267
You're faced with a grave
responsibility. Thank you, gentlemen.

18
00:02:29.576 --> 00:02:31.510
The alternate jurors
are excused.

19
00:02:37.484 --> 00:02:39.418
The jury will now retire.

20
00:05:00.073 --> 00:05:02.633
A piece of gum?
- No, thanks.

21
00:05:05.078 --> 00:05:06.511
Move it.

22
00:05:08.582 --> 00:05:11.107
Give you a hand with that.
- That's it.

23
00:05:17.257 --> 00:05:20.522
You know something? I called
the weather bureau this morning.

24
00:05:20.594 --> 00:05:24.052
This is gonna be the hottest day
of the year.

25
00:05:24.131 --> 00:05:26.292
Boy, you'd think they'd at least
air-condition these places.

26
00:05:26.366 --> 00:05:29.130
What is your name, sir?
- Oh, it's that one. Yeah.

27
00:05:29.202 --> 00:05:33.866
All right. Thank you.
Okay, gentlemen. Everybody's here.

28
00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:36.704
Now, if there's anything you want,
I'll be right outside the door.

29
00:05:36.777 --> 00:05:38.711
Just knock.

30
00:05:51.825 --> 00:05:54.419
Good morning. I never knew
they locked the door.

31
00:05:54.494 --> 00:05:57.327
Sure, they lock the door.
What'd you think?

32
00:05:57.397 --> 00:06:00.298
I don't know. It just
never occurred to me.

33
00:06:01.902 --> 00:06:05.599
What's that for?
- I thought we might vote by ballot.

34
00:06:05.672 --> 00:06:09.608
Great idea. Maybe we can get him
elected senator.

35
00:06:15.982 --> 00:06:19.679
How'd you like it?
- It was pretty interesting.

36
00:06:19.753 --> 00:06:21.687
I almost fell asleep.

37
00:06:21.755 --> 00:06:24.519
I mean, I've never been on
a jury before.

38
00:06:24.591 --> 00:06:28.391
I've sat on many juries.

39
00:06:28.462 --> 00:06:31.260
It gets me the way those lawyers
talk and talk and talk...

40
00:06:31.331 --> 00:06:33.526
even when it's an open and shut case
like this one.

41
00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:35.534
Did you ever hear so much talk
about nothing?

42
00:06:35.602 --> 00:06:40.733
I guess they're entitled.
- They're entitled. It's the system.

43
00:06:40.807 --> 00:06:43.139
But if you ask me,
I'd slap those tough kids down...

44
00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.371
before they start any trouble.

45
00:06:45.445 --> 00:06:48.471
It would save us a lot of time
and money. Let's get started, huh?

46
00:06:48.548 --> 00:06:51.745
Good idea.
- Yeah. We've all got things to do.

47
00:06:51.818 --> 00:06:55.481
We'll start with a five-minute break.
There's one gentleman in the bathroom.

48
00:06:55.555 --> 00:07:00.254
Are we gonna sit in order?
- Gee, I don't know. I guess so.

49
00:07:05.866 --> 00:07:08.858
Pardon me. You're in my seat.
- Oh, excuse me.

50
00:07:11.805 --> 00:07:13.739
Not a bad view, huh?

51
00:07:16.977 --> 00:07:19.445
What did you think of the case?

52
00:07:19.513 --> 00:07:21.606
It had a lot of interest for me.

53
00:07:21.681 --> 00:07:25.310
No real dead spots, you know.

54
00:07:25.385 --> 00:07:28.183
We were lucky to get
a murder case.

55
00:07:28.255 --> 00:07:32.282
I figured us for an assault or burglary.
Boy, they can be the dullest.

56
00:07:35.495 --> 00:07:39.158
Is that the Woolworth Building?
- That's right.

57
00:07:39.232 --> 00:07:43.328
Isn't that funny. I've lived here all
my life and I've never been inside that.

58
00:07:43.403 --> 00:07:47.567
You had to sort out all that junk.
Like that thing with the movies.

59
00:07:47.641 --> 00:07:50.633
You can say that again. What about
that business with the knife?

60
00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:53.702
I mean, asking grown-up people
to believe that kind of jazz.

61
00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:56.271
You can expect that.
You know what we're dealing with.

62
00:07:56.349 --> 00:07:58.874
I guess so.

63
00:07:58.952 --> 00:08:02.854
Your horn works. Now try your lights.
What, do you have a cold?

64
00:08:02.923 --> 00:08:05.983
And how. These hot-weather colds
can kill you.

65
00:08:06.059 --> 00:08:08.619
I can hardly touch my nose. You know?

66
00:08:08.695 --> 00:08:11.994
I sure do. I just got over one.

67
00:08:12.065 --> 00:08:14.966
Come on, Mr. Foreman!
Let's go here, huh?

68
00:08:15.035 --> 00:08:16.969
The guy's still in the bathroom.

69
00:08:17.037 --> 00:08:20.564
What's new? I didn't get to see a paper
this morning.

70
00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:23.108
I was only wondering
how the market closed.

71
00:08:23.176 --> 00:08:25.838
You got a seat on the exchange?

72
00:08:25.912 --> 00:08:27.846
I'm a broker.

73
00:08:27.914 --> 00:08:32.317
I run a messenger service.
The Beck and Call Company.

74
00:08:32.385 --> 00:08:34.444
The name is my wife's idea.

75
00:08:34.521 --> 00:08:38.184
Got 37 men working.
Started with nothing.

76
00:08:38.258 --> 00:08:41.284
Okay, men. Let's take our seats.

77
00:08:44.731 --> 00:08:49.896
We can all get out of here pretty quick.
I have tickets for the ball game.

78
00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:53.497
Yanks and Cleveland. We got this kid
Modjelewski in there.

79
00:08:53.573 --> 00:08:57.441
He's a real bull, this kid.
You know.

80
00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:00.479
A real jug handle. You know.

81
00:09:01.982 --> 00:09:04.746
You're a real baseball fan,
aren't you?

82
00:09:04.818 --> 00:09:08.185
Where do we sit here?
- I thought we'd sit in order.

83
00:09:08.255 --> 00:09:12.191
By jury numbers. One, two, three,
four, five, and so on around the table.

84
00:09:12.259 --> 00:09:14.420
If that's okay with you.
- What's the difference?

85
00:09:14.494 --> 00:09:17.588
It's reasonable to sit in order.
- Let it be.

86
00:09:17.664 --> 00:09:19.598
Number 12 here?

87
00:09:23.436 --> 00:09:27.270
What was your impression
of the prosecuting attorney?

88
00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:30.207
I beg your pardon?
- I thought he was really sharp.

89
00:09:30.277 --> 00:09:36.045
The way he handled those points one by
one, in sequence. I was very impressed.

90
00:09:36.116 --> 00:09:39.176
I think he did an expert job.

91
00:09:39.252 --> 00:09:42.346
A lot of drive too, you know.

92
00:09:42.422 --> 00:09:45.983
Okay, can we hold it down a minute?
Fellows?

93
00:09:46.059 --> 00:09:49.324
Say, we'd like to get started.
Gentleman at the window.

94
00:09:51.231 --> 00:09:53.358
We'd like to get started.
- Oh, I'm sorry.

95
00:09:53.433 --> 00:09:57.130
Tough to figure. Kid kills his father
just like that.

96
00:09:57.203 --> 00:10:02.368
Oh, you see that all the time.
- Those kids run wild up there.

97
00:10:02.442 --> 00:10:04.910
Maybe it serves him right.
Know what I mean?

98
00:10:04.978 --> 00:10:08.345
Is everyone here?
- The old man is inside.

99
00:10:08.415 --> 00:10:10.645
Would you knock on the door for him?

100
00:10:11.851 --> 00:10:15.378
Yankee fan?
- No, Baltimore.

101
00:10:15.455 --> 00:10:16.888
Baltimore?

102
00:10:18.258 --> 00:10:22.422
That's like being hit in the head
with a crow bar once a day.

103
00:10:22.495 --> 00:10:26.329
What've they got? Who've they got
besides good groundskeepers?

104
00:10:26.399 --> 00:10:29.266
We'd like to get started.
- Forgive me, gentlemen.

105
00:10:29.336 --> 00:10:31.736
I didn't mean to keep you waiting.

106
00:10:31.805 --> 00:10:33.670
Baltimore.

107
00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:36.265
Okay, gentlemen.
If I can have your attention.

108
00:10:36.409 --> 00:10:39.572
You can handle this thing any way
that you want to.

109
00:10:39.646 --> 00:10:42.444
I'm not gonna make any rules.

110
00:10:42.515 --> 00:10:45.609
We can discuss it first
and then vote on it.

111
00:10:45.685 --> 00:10:49.246
That's, of course, one way.

112
00:10:49.322 --> 00:10:51.381
And we can vote on it right now...

113
00:10:51.458 --> 00:10:54.052
I think it's customary
to take a preliminary vote.

114
00:10:54.127 --> 00:10:57.358
Yeah, let's vote.
Then maybe we can all get out of here.

115
00:10:59.432 --> 00:11:02.560
Then I think that...

116
00:11:02.635 --> 00:11:05.433
Of course, we have a first degree murder
charge here...

117
00:11:05.505 --> 00:11:07.439
and if we vote the accused guilty...

118
00:11:07.507 --> 00:11:10.806
we've got to send him
to the chair.

119
00:11:12.512 --> 00:11:14.571
That's mandatory.
- I think we know that.

120
00:11:14.647 --> 00:11:16.638
Let's see who's where.

121
00:11:16.716 --> 00:11:20.311
Anyone doesn't want to vote?
- Fine with me.

122
00:11:21.688 --> 00:11:27.092
Then remember that this has to be
12 to nothing, either way.

123
00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:30.220
That's the law.
Are we ready?

124
00:11:30.296 --> 00:11:33.288
All those voting ''guilty,''
please raise your hands.

125
00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:41.734
One, two, three, four, five,
six, seven...

126
00:11:41.808 --> 00:11:44.276
eight, nine, ten, eleven.

127
00:11:44.344 --> 00:11:47.677
Okay, that's 11 ''guilty.''

128
00:11:47.747 --> 00:11:49.681
Those voting ''not guilty''?

129
00:11:50.316 --> 00:11:52.375
One. Right.

130
00:11:52.452 --> 00:11:57.014
Eleven ''guilty,'' one ''not guilty.''
Well, now we know where we are.

131
00:11:57.090 --> 00:12:01.857
Boy, oh, boy. There's always one.

132
00:12:01.928 --> 00:12:06.024
What do we do now?
- I guess we talk.

133
00:12:06.099 --> 00:12:09.330
Oh, boy.
- You really think he's innocent?

134
00:12:13.072 --> 00:12:14.562
I don't know.

135
00:12:14.641 --> 00:12:17.542
You sat in court with the rest of us.

136
00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:19.805
The kid's a dangerous killer.
You could see it.

137
00:12:19.879 --> 00:12:23.280
He's 18 years old.
- That's old enough.

138
00:12:23.349 --> 00:12:26.147
He stabbed his own father
four inches into the chest.

139
00:12:26.219 --> 00:12:28.983
They proved it a dozen different ways.

140
00:12:29.055 --> 00:12:32.513
Would you like me to list them?
- No.

141
00:12:32.592 --> 00:12:34.924
Then what do you want?

142
00:12:34.994 --> 00:12:37.861
I just want to talk.
- What's there to talk about?

143
00:12:37.931 --> 00:12:41.662
Eleven men in here think he's guilty.
Nobody had to think twice except you.

144
00:12:41.734 --> 00:12:44.862
I want to ask you something.
Do you believe his story?

145
00:12:44.938 --> 00:12:47.498
I don't know. Maybe I don't.

146
00:12:47.574 --> 00:12:49.439
So how come you vote ''not guilty''?

147
00:12:49.509 --> 00:12:52.740
There were 11 votes for ''guilty.''
It's not easy to raise my hand...

148
00:12:52.812 --> 00:12:56.339
and send a boy off to die
without talking about it first.

149
00:12:56.416 --> 00:13:00.079
Who says it's easy?
- No one.

150
00:13:00.153 --> 00:13:02.314
Just because I voted fast?

151
00:13:02.388 --> 00:13:04.948
I honestly think the guy's guilty.

152
00:13:05.024 --> 00:13:07.515
Couldn't change my mind
if you talked for a hundred years.

153
00:13:07.594 --> 00:13:10.427
I'm not trying to change your mind.
It's just that...

154
00:13:10.497 --> 00:13:12.658
we're talking about somebody's life
here.

155
00:13:12.732 --> 00:13:14.893
We can't decide it in five minutes.
Supposing we're wrong.

156
00:13:14.968 --> 00:13:18.199
Supposing we're wrong. Supposing this
whole building should fall on my head?

157
00:13:18.271 --> 00:13:21.206
You can suppose anything.
- That's right.

158
00:13:21.274 --> 00:13:22.901
What's the difference how long it takes?

159
00:13:22.976 --> 00:13:25.536
Suppose we do it in five minutes.
So what?

160
00:13:25.612 --> 00:13:29.742
Let's take an hour.
The ball game doesn't start till 8:00.

161
00:13:35.955 --> 00:13:37.684
Who's got something to say?

162
00:13:37.757 --> 00:13:42.251
I'm willing to sit for an hour.
- Great. I heard a pretty good story.

163
00:13:42.328 --> 00:13:44.091
That's not why we're sitting here.

164
00:13:44.163 --> 00:13:49.726
Then you tell me.
What are we sitting here for?

165
00:13:49.802 --> 00:13:52.498
I don't know.
Maybe no reason.

166
00:13:52.572 --> 00:13:56.133
Look. This kid's been kicked around
all of his life.

167
00:13:56.209 --> 00:13:58.143
Born in a slum.

168
00:13:58.211 --> 00:14:01.647
Mother dead since he was nine. He lived
a year and half in an orphanage...

169
00:14:01.714 --> 00:14:05.548
when his father was serving a jail term
for forgery.

170
00:14:05.618 --> 00:14:07.984
That's not a very happy beginning.

171
00:14:08.054 --> 00:14:10.750
He's a wild, angry kid.
That's all he's ever been.

172
00:14:10.823 --> 00:14:15.726
And you know why? Because he's been hit
on the head once a day, every day.

173
00:14:15.795 --> 00:14:18.389
He's had a miserable 18 years.

174
00:14:18.464 --> 00:14:21.126
I just think we owe him
a few words, that's all.

175
00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:24.499
I don't mind telling you this, mister.
We don't owe him a thing.

176
00:14:24.571 --> 00:14:27.665
He got a fair trial, didn't he?
What do you think that trial cost?

177
00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:30.766
He's lucky he got it.
Do you know what I mean?

178
00:14:30.843 --> 00:14:35.246
We're all grown-ups in here.
We heard the facts, didn't we?

179
00:14:35.315 --> 00:14:39.183
You're not gonna tell me to believe
this kid, knowing what he is.

180
00:14:39.252 --> 00:14:43.586
I've lived among them all my life.
You can't believe a word they say.

181
00:14:43.656 --> 00:14:47.114
You know that.
I mean, they're born liars.

182
00:14:47.193 --> 00:14:50.356
Only an ignorant man can believe that.
- Now, listen!

183
00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:53.297
Do you think you were born
with a monopoly on the truth?

184
00:14:53.366 --> 00:14:56.096
I think certain things
should be pointed out to this man.

185
00:14:56.169 --> 00:14:58.160
This isn't Sunday.
We don't need a sermon.

186
00:14:58.237 --> 00:15:01.229
We have a job to do. Let's do it.

187
00:15:02.375 --> 00:15:05.367
Rice Pops. It's a product
I work on at the agency.

188
00:15:05.445 --> 00:15:09.040
The breakfast with a built-in bounce.
I wrote that line.

189
00:15:09.115 --> 00:15:12.016
Very catchy.
- Say, do you mind?

190
00:15:12.085 --> 00:15:16.385
Oh, I'm sorry. I have this habit
of doodling. Keeps me thinking clearly.

191
00:15:16.456 --> 00:15:19.220
We have all this work to do.
No point in staying here forever.

192
00:15:19.292 --> 00:15:21.988
Sorry.
- Okay.

193
00:15:22.061 --> 00:15:25.997
Perhaps if the gentleman
who is disagreeing with us...

194
00:15:26.065 --> 00:15:27.760
perhaps you could tell us why.

195
00:15:27.834 --> 00:15:32.066
Let us know what you're thinking, and we
might show you where you're mixed up.

196
00:15:32.138 --> 00:15:35.232
Look, maybe this is an idea.
I haven't given it much thought.

197
00:15:35.308 --> 00:15:37.936
But it seems to me
that it's up to the group...

198
00:15:38.011 --> 00:15:40.878
to convince this gentleman
that he's wrong and we're right.

199
00:15:40.947 --> 00:15:44.576
Maybe if we each took
a couple of minutes just to...

200
00:15:44.651 --> 00:15:48.052
Well, it was just a quick idea.
- No, no. That's a good one.

201
00:15:48.121 --> 00:15:50.646
Supposing we go once around the table.

202
00:15:50.723 --> 00:15:52.657
I guess you're first.

203
00:15:53.826 --> 00:15:55.760
Well...

204
00:15:57.063 --> 00:16:00.226
It's hard to put into words.

205
00:16:00.299 --> 00:16:02.233
I just think he's guilty.

206
00:16:02.301 --> 00:16:06.260
I thought it was obvious from
the word go. Nobody proved otherwise.

207
00:16:06.339 --> 00:16:10.639
Nobody has to prove otherwise. The
burden of proof's on the prosecution.

208
00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:14.441
The defendant doesn't even have to open
his mouth. That's in the Constitution.

209
00:16:14.514 --> 00:16:16.607
Oh, sure, I know that.

210
00:16:16.683 --> 00:16:19.846
What I meant was...

211
00:16:19.919 --> 00:16:22.183
Well, I just think he's guilty.

212
00:16:22.255 --> 00:16:23.984
I mean, somebody saw him do it.

213
00:16:24.057 --> 00:16:28.824
Okay. Here's what I think,
and I have no personal feelings.

214
00:16:28.895 --> 00:16:32.023
I just want to talk about facts.

215
00:16:32.098 --> 00:16:36.694
Number one. The old man lived
downstairs under the room...

216
00:16:36.769 --> 00:16:38.999
where the killing took place.

217
00:16:39.072 --> 00:16:42.667
At ten minutes after 12:00 that night,
he heard loud noises.

218
00:16:42.742 --> 00:16:44.869
Said it sounded like a fight.

219
00:16:44.944 --> 00:16:47.811
And he heard the kid yell out,
''I'm gonna kill ya.''

220
00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:50.348
A second later he heard a body
hit the floor.

221
00:16:50.416 --> 00:16:54.648
Ran to the door, opened it up, saw the
kid running down and out of the house.

222
00:16:54.721 --> 00:16:58.623
He called the police. They found
the old man with a knife in his chest.

223
00:16:58.691 --> 00:17:01.922
The coroner fixed the time of death
around midnight.

224
00:17:01.994 --> 00:17:03.928
Now, these are facts.

225
00:17:03.996 --> 00:17:07.363
You can't refute facts.
The kid is guilty.

226
00:17:07.433 --> 00:17:10.527
I'm just as sentimental as the next
fellow. I know he's only 18.

227
00:17:10.603 --> 00:17:13.538
But he's still gotta pay
for what he did.

228
00:17:13.606 --> 00:17:17.599
I'm with you.
- Okay. Are you finished?

229
00:17:17.677 --> 00:17:19.110
Yeah.
- Next.

230
00:17:19.178 --> 00:17:22.579
It was obvious to me that the boy's
entire story was flimsy.

231
00:17:22.648 --> 00:17:25.583
He claimed he was at the movies
at the time, yet one hour later...

232
00:17:25.651 --> 00:17:28.677
he couldn't remember the names of
the films he saw or who played in them.

233
00:17:28.755 --> 00:17:31.724
That's right.
- No one saw him at the theater.

234
00:17:31.791 --> 00:17:35.750
What about the woman across the street?
Her testimony proves it.

235
00:17:35.828 --> 00:17:38.854
That's right.
She actually saw the killing.

236
00:17:38.931 --> 00:17:41.832
Please, let's go in order here.
- Just a minute.

237
00:17:41.901 --> 00:17:45.735
Here's a woman who's lying in bed.

238
00:17:45.805 --> 00:17:48.797
She can't sleep.
She's dying with the heat. You know?

239
00:17:48.875 --> 00:17:51.742
She looks out the window,
and right across the street...

240
00:17:51.811 --> 00:17:55.303
she sees the kid stick the knife
into his father.

241
00:17:55.381 --> 00:17:58.782
The time is 12:10 on the nose.
Everything fits.

242
00:17:58.851 --> 00:18:01.046
She's known the kid all his life.

243
00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:05.113
His window is opposite hers across the
El tracks. She swore she saw him do it.

244
00:18:05.191 --> 00:18:07.557
Through the windows
of the passing El train.

245
00:18:07.627 --> 00:18:12.030
This El train had no passengers in it.
Just being moved downtown.

246
00:18:12.098 --> 00:18:13.759
The lights were out, remember?

247
00:18:13.833 --> 00:18:17.428
They proved in court that at night
you can look through the windows...

248
00:18:17.503 --> 00:18:19.767
and see what's happening
on the other side.

249
00:18:19.839 --> 00:18:22.740
They proved it.
- I'd like to ask you something.

250
00:18:22.809 --> 00:18:25.573
You don't believe the boy's story.
How come you believe the woman's?

251
00:18:25.645 --> 00:18:27.203
She's one of ''them'' too, isn't she?

252
00:18:29.982 --> 00:18:31.916
You're a pretty smart fellow,
aren't you?

253
00:18:31.984 --> 00:18:35.886
Okay, gentlemen.
- Sit down.

254
00:18:35.955 --> 00:18:39.322
What's he so wise about?
- Come on.

255
00:18:39.392 --> 00:18:41.917
We're not gonna get anywhere fighting.

256
00:18:41.994 --> 00:18:43.985
Whose turn is it next?

257
00:18:44.063 --> 00:18:45.928
His. Number five.

258
00:18:48.367 --> 00:18:51.803
Well, can I pass?

259
00:18:51.871 --> 00:18:55.068
Well, it's your privilege.

260
00:18:55.141 --> 00:18:59.942
How about the next gentleman?
- Oh, well, I don't know.

261
00:19:00.012 --> 00:19:03.846
I started to be convinced
very early in the case.

262
00:19:04.917 --> 00:19:07.977
I was looking for a motive.

263
00:19:08.054 --> 00:19:11.148
That's very important because
if you don't have a motive...

264
00:19:11.224 --> 00:19:13.385
where's your case, right?

265
00:19:13.459 --> 00:19:17.020
Anyway that testimony from those people
in the apartment...

266
00:19:17.096 --> 00:19:19.530
across the hall
from the kid's apartment...

267
00:19:19.599 --> 00:19:21.760
that was very powerful.

268
00:19:21.834 --> 00:19:24.860
Didn't they say something about a fight,
an argument...

269
00:19:24.937 --> 00:19:27.269
between the old man and his son...

270
00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:29.274
about 7:00 that night?

271
00:19:29.342 --> 00:19:32.675
I could be wrong, but I...
- It was 8:00.

272
00:19:32.745 --> 00:19:35.737
They heard an argument.
They couldn't hear what it was about.

273
00:19:35.815 --> 00:19:38.682
Then they heard the father
hit the boy twice.

274
00:19:38.751 --> 00:19:42.380
Finally they saw the boy run angry
out of the house. What does that prove?

275
00:19:42.455 --> 00:19:45.913
Well, it don't exactly prove anything.
It's just part of the picture.

276
00:19:45.992 --> 00:19:49.120
You said it provided a motive,
and the prosecuting attorney said so.

277
00:19:49.195 --> 00:19:51.527
I don't think that was
a very strong motive.

278
00:19:51.597 --> 00:19:53.531
This boy's been hit so many times...

279
00:19:53.599 --> 00:19:57.797
that violence is practically
a normal state of affairs with him.

280
00:19:57.870 --> 00:20:02.500
I just can't see two slaps in the face
provoking him into committing murder.

281
00:20:02.575 --> 00:20:05.476
It may have been two too many.
Everyone has a breaking point.

282
00:20:08.047 --> 00:20:10.572
Anything else?

283
00:20:10.650 --> 00:20:14.086
No.
- Okay. How about you?

284
00:20:16.489 --> 00:20:19.890
I don't know.
It's all been said.

285
00:20:19.959 --> 00:20:23.793
You can talk here forever.
It's still the same thing.

286
00:20:23.863 --> 00:20:26.832
This kid is 5 for 0.

287
00:20:26.899 --> 00:20:28.799
Look at his record.

288
00:20:28.868 --> 00:20:32.964
At ten he was in children's court.
He threw a rock at a teacher.

289
00:20:33.039 --> 00:20:37.305
When he was 15 he was in reform school.
He stole a car.

290
00:20:37.376 --> 00:20:39.901
He's been arrested for mugging.

291
00:20:39.979 --> 00:20:42.038
He was picked up twice
for knife fighting.

292
00:20:42.114 --> 00:20:44.912
They say he's real handy with a knife.

293
00:20:44.984 --> 00:20:47.009
Oh, this is a very fine boy.

294
00:20:47.086 --> 00:20:50.283
Ever since he was five years old,
his father beat him up regularly.

295
00:20:50.356 --> 00:20:52.586
He used his fists.
- So would I.

296
00:20:52.658 --> 00:20:56.822
A kid like that?
- It's the way these kids are nowadays.

297
00:20:59.799 --> 00:21:03.758
When I was a kid,
I used to call my father ''sir.''

298
00:21:03.836 --> 00:21:07.272
That's right. Do you ever hear a kid
call his father that anymore?

299
00:21:07.340 --> 00:21:10.400
Fathers don't seem to think
it's important anymore.

300
00:21:10.476 --> 00:21:12.944
You got any kids?
- Three.

301
00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:15.914
I got one.

302
00:21:16.983 --> 00:21:18.917
Twenty-two years old.

303
00:21:24.623 --> 00:21:29.117
When he was nine years old
he ran away from a fight.

304
00:21:29.195 --> 00:21:32.323
I saw it. I was so embarrassed,
I almost threw up.

305
00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:38.799
I said, ''I'm gonna make a man out of you
if I have to break you in two trying.''

306
00:21:41.073 --> 00:21:43.974
Well, I made a man out of him.

307
00:21:44.043 --> 00:21:47.672
When he was 16 we had a fight.

308
00:21:47.747 --> 00:21:51.615
He hit me in the jaw.
He was a big kid.

309
00:21:53.519 --> 00:21:55.714
I haven't seen him
for two years.

310
00:21:58.824 --> 00:22:01.349
Kids.

311
00:22:01.427 --> 00:22:03.361
You work your heart out...

312
00:22:05.765 --> 00:22:07.198
Let's get going.

313
00:22:07.266 --> 00:22:09.894
I think we're missing the point here.

314
00:22:09.969 --> 00:22:13.370
Let's say this boy is the product of
a broken home and a filthy neighborhood.

315
00:22:13.439 --> 00:22:16.966
We can't help that. We're here to decide
whether he's innocent or guilty...

316
00:22:17.043 --> 00:22:19.807
not to go into the reasons
why he grew up the way he did.

317
00:22:19.879 --> 00:22:22.871
He was born in a slum. Slums are
breeding grounds for criminals.

318
00:22:22.948 --> 00:22:26.907
We know that. It's no secret that
children from slum backgrounds...

319
00:22:26.986 --> 00:22:29.546
are potential menaces
to society.

320
00:22:29.622 --> 00:22:31.817
Now, I think...
- Brother, you can say that again.

321
00:22:31.891 --> 00:22:34.485
The kids who crawl out of these places
are real trash.

322
00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:38.052
I don't want any part of them.
- Listen, mister.

323
00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:41.395
I've lived in a slum all my life.
- Wait a minute...

324
00:22:41.467 --> 00:22:44.868
I played in backyards
that were filled with garbage.

325
00:22:44.937 --> 00:22:47.735
Maybe you can still smell it on me.
- Now, listen, sonny.

326
00:22:47.807 --> 00:22:52.039
There's nothing personal.
- He didn't mean you.

327
00:22:52.111 --> 00:22:53.976
Let's not be so sensitive.

328
00:22:54.046 --> 00:22:56.537
This sensitivity I can understand.

329
00:22:56.615 --> 00:23:01.746
Okay. Look. Let's stop the arguing.
We're only wasting time.

330
00:23:01.821 --> 00:23:05.154
Your turn down there.
Let's go.

331
00:23:05.224 --> 00:23:09.058
I didn't expect a turn. I thought you
were all trying to convince me.

332
00:23:09.128 --> 00:23:11.756
Check. That was the idea.
- I forgot about that. He's right.

333
00:23:11.831 --> 00:23:14.766
What's the difference? He's the one
keeping us in here. Let's hear from him.

334
00:23:14.834 --> 00:23:19.203
Wait. We decided to do this a certain
way. We ought to stick to that way.

335
00:23:19.271 --> 00:23:21.262
Stop being a kid, will you?

336
00:23:21.340 --> 00:23:26.437
What do you mean?
- What do you think I mean? K-I-D, kid.

337
00:23:28.614 --> 00:23:31.447
Just because I'm trying to keep
this thing organized?

338
00:23:31.517 --> 00:23:33.644
Here, you take it.
You take on the responsibility.

339
00:23:33.719 --> 00:23:37.780
I'll keep my mouth shut. That's all.
- What are you so hot about? Calm down.

340
00:23:37.857 --> 00:23:41.657
Don't tell me to calm down. You want to
take the chair, just take the chair.

341
00:23:41.727 --> 00:23:45.060
Did you ever see such a thing?
- You think it's funny?

342
00:23:45.131 --> 00:23:47.565
Forget it. The whole thing's
unimportant.

343
00:23:47.633 --> 00:23:49.328
Unimportant? You try it.

344
00:23:49.401 --> 00:23:52.632
Nobody wants to change.
You're doing a beautiful job. Sit down.

345
00:23:52.705 --> 00:23:57.233
Yeah, you're doing great.
Just stay in there and pitch.

346
00:23:58.611 --> 00:24:01.978
All right. Let's hear from somebody.

347
00:24:02.047 --> 00:24:05.949
If you want me to tell you
how I feel, it's all right with me.

348
00:24:06.018 --> 00:24:11.320
Boy, I don't care what you do.
- I don't have anything brilliant.

349
00:24:11.390 --> 00:24:13.654
I only know as much as you do.

350
00:24:13.726 --> 00:24:17.355
According to the testimony,
the boy looks guilty. Maybe he is.

351
00:24:17.429 --> 00:24:22.162
I sat there in court for six days
while the evidence built up.

352
00:24:22.234 --> 00:24:26.603
Everybody sounded so positive. I got
a peculiar feeling about this trial.

353
00:24:26.672 --> 00:24:29.197
I mean, nothing is that positive.

354
00:24:29.275 --> 00:24:31.209
There's questions
I'd have liked to ask.

355
00:24:31.277 --> 00:24:34.610
Maybe they wouldn't have meant anything.
But I began to get the feeling...

356
00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:38.377
that the defense counsel wasn't
conducting a thorough cross examination.

357
00:24:38.450 --> 00:24:41.783
He let too many things go by.
Little things.

358
00:24:41.854 --> 00:24:44.721
What little things? When these fellows
don't ask questions...

359
00:24:44.790 --> 00:24:47.020
it's because they know the answers
already and they figured they'd be hurt.

360
00:24:47.092 --> 00:24:52.428
Maybe it's also possible for a lawyer
to be just plain stupid, isn't it?

361
00:24:52.498 --> 00:24:55.160
You sound like you met
my brother-in-law once.

362
00:24:57.503 --> 00:24:59.937
I kept putting myself
in the kid's place.

363
00:25:01.941 --> 00:25:04.739
I'd have asked for another lawyer,
I think.

364
00:25:04.810 --> 00:25:07.677
If I was on trial for my life,
I'd want him...

365
00:25:07.746 --> 00:25:11.614
to tear the prosecution witnesses
to shreds, or at least try to.

366
00:25:11.684 --> 00:25:16.121
Look. There was one alleged eyewitness
to this killing.

367
00:25:16.188 --> 00:25:19.589
Someone claims he heard the killing,
saw the boy run out afterwards...

368
00:25:19.658 --> 00:25:21.853
and there was a lot of circumstantial
evidence.

369
00:25:21.927 --> 00:25:25.863
But actually those two witnesses were
the entire case for the prosecution.

370
00:25:25.931 --> 00:25:29.162
Supposing they're wrong.
- What do you mean?

371
00:25:29.235 --> 00:25:33.035
What's the point of having witnesses?
- Could they be wrong?

372
00:25:33.105 --> 00:25:35.471
What are you trying to say? Those people
sat on the stand under oath.

373
00:25:35.541 --> 00:25:38.567
They're only people. People
make mistakes. Could they be wrong?

374
00:25:39.778 --> 00:25:42.645
No, I don't think so.
- Do you know so?

375
00:25:42.715 --> 00:25:46.116
Nobody can know a thing like that.
This isn't an exact science.

376
00:25:46.185 --> 00:25:47.438
That's right. It isn't.

377
00:25:52.844 --> 00:25:55.404
Okay. Let's get to the point.

378
00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:58.244
What about the switch knife
they found in the old man's chest?

379
00:25:58.316 --> 00:26:01.547
Wait. There's some people who haven't
talked yet. Shouldn't we go in order?

380
00:26:01.619 --> 00:26:05.146
They'll get a chance to talk.
Be quiet a second, will you?

381
00:26:05.223 --> 00:26:10.388
What about this knife this fine,
upright boy admitted buying that night?

382
00:26:10.461 --> 00:26:12.895
Let's talk about it.
- All right.

383
00:26:12.964 --> 00:26:15.455
Let's get it in here and look at it.
I'd like to see it again, Mr. Foreman.

384
00:26:15.533 --> 00:26:19.060
We all saw what it looks like.
Why do we have to see it again?

385
00:26:19.137 --> 00:26:21.799
The gentleman has a right to see
exhibits in evidence.

386
00:26:23.374 --> 00:26:26.172
Say, could you bring us the knife?
- Right.

387
00:26:27.879 --> 00:26:30.746
The knife and the way it was bought
is pretty strong evidence.

388
00:26:30.815 --> 00:26:32.248
Don't you think?
- I do.

389
00:26:32.317 --> 00:26:35.718
Good. Suppose we take these facts
one at a time.

390
00:26:35.787 --> 00:26:38.847
One. The boy admitted going
out of the house at 8:00 that night...

391
00:26:38.923 --> 00:26:41.619
after being slapped several times
by his father.

392
00:26:41.693 --> 00:26:46.357
No, he didn't say ''slapped.''
He said ''punched.'' There's a difference.

393
00:26:46.431 --> 00:26:49.059
After being hit several times
by his father.

394
00:26:49.133 --> 00:26:52.967
Two. He went directly to a neighborhood
junk shop where he bought one of those...

395
00:26:53.037 --> 00:26:55.665
Switch knives.
- Switchblade knives.

396
00:26:55.740 --> 00:27:00.404
This wasn't an ordinary knife. It had
an unusual carved handle and blade.

397
00:27:00.478 --> 00:27:05.040
The storekeeper said it was the only one
of its kind he'd ever had in stock.

398
00:27:05.116 --> 00:27:10.383
Three. He met friends in front of the
tavern about 8:45. Am I right so far?

399
00:27:10.455 --> 00:27:12.616
Yes.
- You bet he is.

400
00:27:12.690 --> 00:27:15.887
He talked with his friends for about
an hour, leaving them at 9:45.

401
00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:18.986
During this time,
they saw the switch knife.

402
00:27:19.063 --> 00:27:20.724
Four.

403
00:27:20.798 --> 00:27:25.929
They identified the death weapon
in court as that very same knife.

404
00:27:26.004 --> 00:27:28.734
Five. He arrived home at about 10:00.

405
00:27:28.806 --> 00:27:33.209
This is where the stories offered by
the State and the boy diverge slightly.

406
00:27:33.277 --> 00:27:35.939
He claims that he went to a movie
about 11:30...

407
00:27:36.014 --> 00:27:40.144
returning home at 3:10 to find
his father dead and himself arrested.

408
00:27:40.218 --> 00:27:44.484
He also claims the two detectives
threw him down half a flight of stairs.

409
00:27:44.555 --> 00:27:46.386
What happened to the switch knife?

410
00:27:46.457 --> 00:27:49.221
He claims that it fell through a hole in
his pocket on the way to the movies...

411
00:27:49.293 --> 00:27:54.094
sometime between 11:30 and 3:10,
and that he never saw it again.

412
00:27:54.165 --> 00:27:55.996
Now, there is a tale, gentlemen.

413
00:27:56.067 --> 00:27:59.503
I think that it's quite clear that the
boy never went to the movies that night.

414
00:27:59.570 --> 00:28:03.097
No one in the house saw him go out.
No one at the theater identified him.

415
00:28:03.174 --> 00:28:06.337
He couldn't remember the names
of the pictures he saw.

416
00:28:08.246 --> 00:28:10.180
What actually happened is this.

417
00:28:10.248 --> 00:28:13.445
The boy stayed home, had another fight
with his father...

418
00:28:13.518 --> 00:28:17.249
stabbed him to death and left the house
at ten minutes after 12:00.

419
00:28:17.321 --> 00:28:20.119
He even remembered to wipe the knife
clean of fingerprints.

420
00:28:20.191 --> 00:28:22.489
Are you trying to tell me this knife...

421
00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:24.994
really fell through a hole
in his pocket?

422
00:28:25.063 --> 00:28:27.190
Someone picked it up,
went to the boy's house...

423
00:28:27.265 --> 00:28:29.995
and stabbed his father with it
just to test its sharpness?

424
00:28:30.068 --> 00:28:32.195
No, I'm just saying it's possible
the boy lost his knife...

425
00:28:32.270 --> 00:28:34.534
and somebody else stabbed his father
with a similar knife.

426
00:28:34.605 --> 00:28:37.039
It's possible.
- Look at this knife.

427
00:28:39.310 --> 00:28:41.437
It's a very unusual knife.

428
00:28:41.512 --> 00:28:45.949
I've never seen one like it.
Neither had the storekeeper who sold it.

429
00:28:46.017 --> 00:28:48.713
Aren't you asking us to accept
a pretty incredible coincidence?

430
00:28:48.786 --> 00:28:51.152
I'm just saying a coincidence
is possible.

431
00:28:51.222 --> 00:28:52.712
And I say it's not possible.

432
00:28:59.831 --> 00:29:02.925
Where did that come from?
- It's the same knife!

433
00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:05.195
What are you doing?
- Where did you get it?

434
00:29:06.137 --> 00:29:10.471
I went out walking last night.
I walked through the boy's neighborhood.

435
00:29:10.541 --> 00:29:14.875
I bought that at a pawn shop two blocks
from the boy's house. It cost $6.

436
00:29:16.013 --> 00:29:18.072
It's against the law
to buy or sell switchblade knives.

437
00:29:18.149 --> 00:29:20.413
That's right. I broke the law.

438
00:29:20.485 --> 00:29:24.717
You pulled a real bright trick.
Now tell me what it proves.

439
00:29:24.789 --> 00:29:27.815
Maybe there are ten knives like that.
- Maybe there are.

440
00:29:27.892 --> 00:29:29.757
What does it mean?

441
00:29:29.827 --> 00:29:32.921
You found a knife like it. What's that?
The discovery of the age or something?

442
00:29:32.997 --> 00:29:35.465
Are you asking us to believe that
somebody else did the stabbing...

443
00:29:35.533 --> 00:29:37.228
with exactly the same kind of knife?

444
00:29:37.301 --> 00:29:40.293
The odds are a million to one.
- It's possible.

445
00:29:40.371 --> 00:29:42.771
But not very probable.

446
00:29:42.840 --> 00:29:47.174
Okay. Let's take our seats. There's
no point in standing around all over.

447
00:29:48.646 --> 00:29:51.945
It's interesting that he'd find a knife
exactly like the boy's.

448
00:29:52.016 --> 00:29:55.008
What's interesting about it?

449
00:29:55.086 --> 00:29:59.182
I just thought it was interesting.
- There are 11 who think he's guilty.

450
00:29:59.257 --> 00:30:02.886
Right. What are you accomplishing?
You're not gonna change anybody's mind.

451
00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:05.394
If you want to be stubborn and hang
this jury, go ahead.

452
00:30:05.463 --> 00:30:10.196
The kid will be tried again
and found guilty as sure as he's born.

453
00:30:10.268 --> 00:30:12.031
You're probably right.

454
00:30:12.103 --> 00:30:15.197
So what are you gonna do?
We could be here all night.

455
00:30:15.273 --> 00:30:18.606
It's only one night.
A boy may die.

456
00:30:18.676 --> 00:30:21.236
Why don't we just set up house here?

457
00:30:21.312 --> 00:30:23.837
Someone send Potsy the policeman
to get a pinochle deck...

458
00:30:23.915 --> 00:30:27.681
and we'll just sweat the whole thing out
right here.

459
00:30:27.752 --> 00:30:30.949
I don't think he should joke about it.
- What do you want me to do about it?

460
00:30:31.022 --> 00:30:34.719
I don't see what all this stuff about
the knife has to do with anything.

461
00:30:34.792 --> 00:30:37.761
Somebody saw the kid stab his father.
What more do we need?

462
00:30:37.829 --> 00:30:40.559
You can talk the ears right off my head.

463
00:30:40.631 --> 00:30:45.068
I got three garages going to pot
while you're talking! Let's get done!

464
00:30:45.136 --> 00:30:48.902
The knife was important to the district
attorney. He spent one whole day.

465
00:30:48.973 --> 00:30:51.498
He's a 15th assistant or something.
What does he know about it?

466
00:30:51.576 --> 00:30:56.013
Let's hold it down. These side arguments
are only slowing us up.

467
00:30:57.982 --> 00:31:02.112
Well, what about it?
You're the only one.

468
00:31:08.759 --> 00:31:11.887
I have a proposition to make
to all of you.

469
00:31:11.963 --> 00:31:15.023
I'm gonna call for another vote.

470
00:31:15.099 --> 00:31:20.162
I want you 11 men to vote
by secret written ballot.

471
00:31:20.238 --> 00:31:22.263
I'll abstain.

472
00:31:22.340 --> 00:31:25.366
If there are 11 votes for ''guilty,''
I won't stand alone.

473
00:31:25.443 --> 00:31:29.004
We'll take in a guilty verdict
to the judge right now.

474
00:31:30.581 --> 00:31:34.642
But if anyone votes ''not guilty,''
we'll stay here and talk it out.

475
00:31:37.722 --> 00:31:40.452
That's it. If you want to try it,
I'm ready.

476
00:31:40.524 --> 00:31:42.617
All right. Let's do it the hard way.

477
00:31:42.693 --> 00:31:47.255
That sounds fair.
Anyone doesn't agree? Pass these along.

478
00:32:21.399 --> 00:32:23.333
Guilty.

479
00:32:51.595 --> 00:32:55.326
Not guilty. Guilty.

480
00:32:55.399 --> 00:32:59.529
How do you like that!
- Another chap flips his wings.

481
00:32:59.603 --> 00:33:02.367
All right. Who was it?
I wanna know.

482
00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:04.738
Excuse me. This was a secret ballot.

483
00:33:04.809 --> 00:33:09.143
We all agreed on that. If the gentleman
wants it to remain secret...

484
00:33:09.213 --> 00:33:14.549
Secret! There are no secrets
in a jury room. I know who it was.

485
00:33:14.618 --> 00:33:16.609
Brother, you really are something.

486
00:33:16.687 --> 00:33:18.655
You sit here, vote ''guilty''
like the rest of us...

487
00:33:18.723 --> 00:33:22.124
and some golden-voiced preacher
tears your heart out...

488
00:33:22.193 --> 00:33:25.458
about some underprivileged kid
just couldn't help becoming a murderer!

489
00:33:25.529 --> 00:33:29.192
And you change your vote.
If that isn't sickening.

490
00:33:29.266 --> 00:33:32.895
Why don't you drop a quarter
in his collection box?

491
00:33:32.970 --> 00:33:34.995
Just wait a minute.

492
00:33:35.072 --> 00:33:38.508
You can't talk to me like that.
Who do you think you are?

493
00:33:38.576 --> 00:33:42.137
Calm down. It doesn't matter.
He's very excitable.

494
00:33:42.213 --> 00:33:44.204
Excitable! You bet I'm excitable!

495
00:33:44.281 --> 00:33:47.512
We're trying to put a guilty man
in the chair, where he belongs!

496
00:33:47.585 --> 00:33:51.316
Someone starts telling fairy tales,
and we're listening!

497
00:33:51.389 --> 00:33:54.586
Come on.
- What made you change your vote?

498
00:33:54.658 --> 00:33:56.956
He didn't change his vote.
I did.

499
00:33:57.028 --> 00:33:59.019
Oh, fine.
- I knew it.

500
00:33:59.096 --> 00:34:01.030
Would you like me to tell you why?

501
00:34:01.098 --> 00:34:02.998
No, I wouldn't like you
to tell me why.

502
00:34:03.067 --> 00:34:05.763
I'd like to make it clear anyway,
if you don't mind.

503
00:34:05.836 --> 00:34:09.169
Do we have to listen to this?
- The man wants to talk.

504
00:34:09.240 --> 00:34:14.200
Thank you. This gentleman has been
standing alone against us.

505
00:34:14.278 --> 00:34:18.647
He doesn't say the boy is not guilty.
He just isn't sure.

506
00:34:18.716 --> 00:34:21.651
It's not easy to stand alone
against the ridicule of others...

507
00:34:21.719 --> 00:34:24.745
so he gambled for support,
and I gave it to him.

508
00:34:24.822 --> 00:34:28.417
I respect his motives.
The boy on trial is probably guilty.

509
00:34:28.492 --> 00:34:34.124
But I want to hear more. Right now the
vote is ten to two. I'm talking here!

510
00:34:34.198 --> 00:34:37.326
You have no right to leave this room.
- He can't hear you. He never will.

511
00:34:37.401 --> 00:34:39.335
Let's sit down.

512
00:34:43.574 --> 00:34:45.371
Shall we continue?

513
00:34:45.443 --> 00:34:47.968
Well, I think we ought to take
a break.

514
00:34:48.045 --> 00:34:51.811
One man's inside, and I think
we ought to wait for him.

515
00:34:55.219 --> 00:34:57.949
Looks like we're really hung up here.

516
00:34:58.022 --> 00:35:00.422
That thing with the old man,
that was pretty unexpected.

517
00:35:02.726 --> 00:35:05.752
Wish I could figure out some way
we could break it up.

518
00:35:08.132 --> 00:35:12.501
You know, in advertising...
I told you I worked in an agency?

519
00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:16.631
There's some pretty strange people
there. Well, not strange, really.

520
00:35:16.707 --> 00:35:20.734
They just have peculiar ways
of expressing themselves.

521
00:35:20.811 --> 00:35:24.008
I suppose it's the same in your business
too, huh?

522
00:35:24.081 --> 00:35:26.743
What do you do?
- I'm a watchmaker.

523
00:35:26.817 --> 00:35:30.753
Really? I imagine the finest watchmakers
in the world come from Europe.

524
00:35:30.821 --> 00:35:34.222
Anyway, in an agency,
when we reach a point like this...

525
00:35:34.291 --> 00:35:37.852
I'm telling about in an ad agency when
we reach a point like this in a meeting.

526
00:35:37.928 --> 00:35:42.558
There's always some character
ready with an idea. And it kills me.

527
00:35:42.633 --> 00:35:47.002
It's the weirdest thing how they
precede their idea with a phrase.

528
00:35:47.071 --> 00:35:50.563
Like, some account executive
will get up, and he'll say...

529
00:35:50.641 --> 00:35:54.543
''Okay. Here's an idea.
Let's run it up the flagpole...

530
00:35:54.612 --> 00:35:56.603
and see if anyone salutes it.''

531
00:35:56.680 --> 00:35:59.342
It's idiotic, but it's funny.

532
00:35:59.416 --> 00:36:04.820
I got a little excited back there.

533
00:36:04.889 --> 00:36:07.585
I didn't mean to get nasty.

534
00:36:07.658 --> 00:36:12.459
Glad you're not one of those that lets
these emotional appeals influence you.

535
00:36:28.345 --> 00:36:30.643
I don't know what's the matter
with that fan. Can't get it started.

536
00:36:40.591 --> 00:36:42.821
Are you a salesman?

537
00:36:42.893 --> 00:36:44.827
I'm an architect.

538
00:36:46.597 --> 00:36:48.531
You know what the ''soft sell'' is?

539
00:36:51.802 --> 00:36:55.238
Well, you got it, believe me.

540
00:36:55.306 --> 00:36:57.740
I got a different technique.

541
00:36:57.808 --> 00:37:02.802
Laughs, drinks, jokes, tricks.
You know?

542
00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:08.113
Yeah. Hit 'em where they live.
That's my motto.

543
00:37:09.286 --> 00:37:12.778
I made 27 grand last year
selling marmalade.

544
00:37:12.856 --> 00:37:15.450
That's not bad,
considering marmalade.

545
00:37:18.562 --> 00:37:20.689
What are you getting out of this?
Kicks?

546
00:37:22.433 --> 00:37:25.698
Or did somebody bump you on the head
one time and you haven't gotten over it.

547
00:37:25.769 --> 00:37:27.703
Maybe.

548
00:37:27.771 --> 00:37:30.262
You do-gooders are all alike.

549
00:37:30.341 --> 00:37:33.003
You're always blowing your stacks
over some guy who fanned.

550
00:37:36.180 --> 00:37:38.740
What're you wasting our time for?

551
00:37:38.816 --> 00:37:43.048
Why don't you donate five dollars to
the cause, and maybe you'll feel better?

552
00:37:46.223 --> 00:37:48.384
This kid is guilty, pal.

553
00:37:48.459 --> 00:37:50.950
It's as plain as the nose
on your face.

554
00:37:51.028 --> 00:37:53.223
So why don't we stop wasting
our time here?

555
00:37:53.297 --> 00:37:56.061
We'll all get sore throats.

556
00:37:56.133 --> 00:37:59.432
What difference does it make
if you get it here or at the ball game?

557
00:37:59.503 --> 00:38:04.133
No difference, pal.
No difference at all.

558
00:38:16.453 --> 00:38:18.216
Nice bunch of guys, huh?

559
00:38:18.289 --> 00:38:21.520
They're about the same as anyone else.

560
00:38:21.592 --> 00:38:24.459
Boy, what a murderous day.

561
00:38:24.528 --> 00:38:28.396
You think we'll be much longer?
- I don't know.

562
00:38:28.465 --> 00:38:31.730
He's guilty for sure.
Not a doubt in the world.

563
00:38:31.802 --> 00:38:34.327
We should have been done already.

564
00:38:36.707 --> 00:38:39.141
Oh, I don't mind, you know.

565
00:38:39.209 --> 00:38:41.143
Beats working.

566
00:38:47.685 --> 00:38:49.744
You think he's not guilty?

567
00:38:49.820 --> 00:38:52.311
I don't know. It's possible.

568
00:38:52.389 --> 00:38:56.223
I don't know you, but I bet you've
never been wronger in your life.

569
00:38:56.293 --> 00:38:58.761
You're wasting your time.
You ought to wrap it up.

570
00:38:58.829 --> 00:39:01.730
Supposing you were the one on trial.

571
00:39:01.799 --> 00:39:05.030
Well, I'm not used to supposing.
I'm just a working man.

572
00:39:05.102 --> 00:39:09.903
My boss does the supposing.
But I'll try one.

573
00:39:11.008 --> 00:39:13.203
Supposing you talk us all out
of this...

574
00:39:13.277 --> 00:39:16.906
and the kid really did knife
his father.

575
00:39:29.193 --> 00:39:32.788
Sorry, blue eyes.
Well, where else?

576
00:39:32.863 --> 00:39:34.854
Let's take our seats.

577
00:39:34.932 --> 00:39:37.162
Looks like we'll be here
for dinner, huh?

578
00:39:37.234 --> 00:39:41.034
Okay. Let's get down to business.
Who wants to start it off?

579
00:39:41.105 --> 00:39:42.663
I will.
- Okay. Go.

580
00:39:42.740 --> 00:39:46.267
You, down there. The old man
who lived downstairs...

581
00:39:46.343 --> 00:39:48.675
says he heard the kid yell out,
''I'm going to kill ya.''

582
00:39:48.746 --> 00:39:51.306
A second later he heard the body
hit the floor.

583
00:39:51.382 --> 00:39:55.318
He ran to the door, and he saw the kid
running down the stairs and out.

584
00:39:55.386 --> 00:39:57.980
What does that mean?
- I was wondering how clearly...

585
00:39:58.055 --> 00:40:00.319
the old man could have heard
the boy through the ceiling.

586
00:40:00.391 --> 00:40:04.384
The window was open. So was the one
upstairs. It was a hot night.

587
00:40:04.461 --> 00:40:08.397
It was another apartment. It's not
that easy to identify a shouting voice.

588
00:40:08.465 --> 00:40:11.662
He identified it in court.
- Remember the lady across the street.

589
00:40:11.735 --> 00:40:14.568
She looked right in the open window
and saw the boy stab his father.

590
00:40:14.638 --> 00:40:17.129
Isn't that enough for you?
- No, it isn't.

591
00:40:17.207 --> 00:40:21.007
Boy, how do you like this guy?
It's like talking into a dead phone.

592
00:40:21.078 --> 00:40:24.241
She said she saw the killing through
the windows of a moving elevated train.

593
00:40:24.314 --> 00:40:27.772
There were six cars. She saw the killing
through the last two cars.

594
00:40:27.851 --> 00:40:31.947
She remembered the most insignificant
details. You can't argue with that.

595
00:40:32.022 --> 00:40:37.187
Has anybody here any idea how long
it would take an el...

596
00:40:46.770 --> 00:40:48.397
This isn't a game.

597
00:40:52.709 --> 00:40:54.836
Did you see him?

598
00:40:54.912 --> 00:40:56.709
The nerve.
- All right. Listen.

599
00:40:56.780 --> 00:40:59.943
The absolute nerve!
- Forget it. It's not important.

600
00:41:00.017 --> 00:41:03.953
This isn't a game!
Who does he think he is?

601
00:41:05.656 --> 00:41:08.921
Has anybody any idea how long it takes
an elevated train...

602
00:41:08.992 --> 00:41:10.926
going at medium speed
to pass a given point?

603
00:41:10.994 --> 00:41:12.859
What has that to do with anything?
- How long? Take a guess.

604
00:41:12.930 --> 00:41:15.626
I haven't the slightest idea.
- What do you think?

605
00:41:15.699 --> 00:41:18.361
Maybe ten, twelve seconds.

606
00:41:18.435 --> 00:41:20.266
I think that's a good guess.
Anyone else?

607
00:41:20.337 --> 00:41:23.101
That sounds right.
- What's the guessing game for?

608
00:41:23.173 --> 00:41:25.038
You?
- Ten seconds is about right.

609
00:41:25.108 --> 00:41:26.939
Ten seconds. What are you getting at?

610
00:41:27.010 --> 00:41:32.141
This. It takes a six-car El train
ten seconds to pass a given point.

611
00:41:32.216 --> 00:41:36.516
Let's say the point is the open window
where the killing took place.

612
00:41:36.587 --> 00:41:39.886
You can reach out of that window
and almost touch the El tracks, right?

613
00:41:39.957 --> 00:41:43.757
Let me ask you this. Has anyone here
ever lived near the El tracks?

614
00:41:43.827 --> 00:41:47.661
I just finished painting an apartment
that overlooked an El line.

615
00:41:47.731 --> 00:41:50.131
I was there for three days.
- What was it like? Noisy?

616
00:41:50.200 --> 00:41:53.135
Oh, brother. No matter, though.
We were all punchy.

617
00:41:53.203 --> 00:41:55.831
I lived in a second-floor apartment
near the El line once.

618
00:41:55.906 --> 00:41:58.932
When the window is open and the train
goes by, the noise is almost unbearable.

619
00:41:59.009 --> 00:42:02.843
You can hardly hear yourself think.
- Will you get to the point?

620
00:42:02.913 --> 00:42:08.078
Just a minute. Let's take two pieces
of testimony and put them together.

621
00:42:08.151 --> 00:42:12.349
First, the old man in the apartment
downstairs.

622
00:42:12.422 --> 00:42:14.913
He says he heard the boy say,
''I'm gonna kill you...''

623
00:42:14.992 --> 00:42:17.722
and a split second later
heard a body hit the floor.

624
00:42:17.794 --> 00:42:22.527
One second later, right?
Second. The woman across the street...

625
00:42:22.599 --> 00:42:25.432
swore positively she looked
out of the window...

626
00:42:25.502 --> 00:42:29.871
and saw the killing through the last
two cars of the passing train.

627
00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:32.670
What are you giving us here?
- Just a minute.

628
00:42:32.743 --> 00:42:37.942
We've agreed that it takes ten seconds
for a train to pass a point.

629
00:42:38.015 --> 00:42:41.348
Since the woman saw the killing
through the last two cars...

630
00:42:41.418 --> 00:42:46.583
we can assume that the body hit
the floor just as the train went by.

631
00:42:46.657 --> 00:42:50.957
Therefore the train had been roaring
by the old man's window...

632
00:42:51.028 --> 00:42:54.555
a full ten seconds before the body
hit the floor.

633
00:42:54.631 --> 00:42:57.600
The old man, according to his own
testimony, ''I'm gonna kill you...''

634
00:42:57.668 --> 00:42:59.932
body hitting the floor
a split second later...

635
00:43:00.003 --> 00:43:03.769
would have had to hear the boy make this
statement with the El roaring past.

636
00:43:03.840 --> 00:43:05.569
He couldn't have heard it.

637
00:43:05.642 --> 00:43:08.236
That's idiotic.
- Do you think he could have heard it?

638
00:43:08.312 --> 00:43:10.906
He said he yelled at the top of his
voice. That's good enough for me.

639
00:43:10.981 --> 00:43:14.075
Even if he heard, he couldn't have
identified the voice with the roaring.

640
00:43:14.151 --> 00:43:16.847
You're talking about seconds.
Nobody can be that accurate.

641
00:43:16.920 --> 00:43:21.619
Testimony that could put a boy into the
electric chair should be that accurate.

642
00:43:23.894 --> 00:43:28.058
I don't think he could have heard it.
- Maybe he didn't, with that El noise.

643
00:43:28.131 --> 00:43:29.996
What are you people talking about?

644
00:43:30.067 --> 00:43:32.092
It stands to reason
he couldn't have heard it.

645
00:43:32.169 --> 00:43:34.137
Why should he lie?
What's he got to gain?

646
00:43:34.204 --> 00:43:36.934
Attention, maybe.

647
00:43:37.007 --> 00:43:39.567
You keep coming in with these bright
sayings! Send them in to a paper!

648
00:43:39.643 --> 00:43:41.577
They pay three dollars apiece.

649
00:43:41.645 --> 00:43:44.580
What are you talking to him
like that for?

650
00:43:44.648 --> 00:43:48.106
A guy talks like that to an old man
really ought to get stepped on.

651
00:43:48.185 --> 00:43:51.177
You ought to have more respect,
mister.

652
00:43:51.254 --> 00:43:55.691
If you say stuff like that to him again,
I'm gonna lay you out.

653
00:44:06.003 --> 00:44:08.665
Now, you go ahead.
You say anything you like.

654
00:44:09.773 --> 00:44:12.867
Why do you think the old man
might lie?

655
00:44:12.943 --> 00:44:16.936
It was just that I looked at him
for a very long time.

656
00:44:17.014 --> 00:44:20.347
Seam of his jacket was split
under the shoulder.

657
00:44:20.417 --> 00:44:24.649
Did you notice that? I mean,
to come to court like that.

658
00:44:27.557 --> 00:44:31.288
He was a very old man
in a torn jacket...

659
00:44:31.361 --> 00:44:34.888
and he walked very slowly to the stand.

660
00:44:34.965 --> 00:44:39.061
He was dragging his left leg
and trying to hide it...

661
00:44:39.136 --> 00:44:41.798
because he was ashamed.

662
00:44:41.872 --> 00:44:46.206
I think I know this man
better than anyone here.

663
00:44:46.276 --> 00:44:50.645
This is a quiet, frightened,
insignificant old man...

664
00:44:50.714 --> 00:44:54.343
who has been nothing all of his life...

665
00:44:54.418 --> 00:44:59.355
who has never had recognition
or his name in the newspapers.

666
00:44:59.423 --> 00:45:01.789
Nobody knows him.

667
00:45:01.858 --> 00:45:03.826
Nobody quotes him.

668
00:45:03.894 --> 00:45:08.524
Nobody seeks his advice
after 75 years.

669
00:45:08.598 --> 00:45:13.194
Gentlemen, that's a very sad thing
to mean nothing.

670
00:45:13.270 --> 00:45:16.034
A man like this needs to be quoted...

671
00:45:16.106 --> 00:45:20.167
to be listened to,
to be quoted just once.

672
00:45:20.243 --> 00:45:22.507
Very important to him.

673
00:45:22.579 --> 00:45:25.480
It would be so hard for him
to recede into the background.

674
00:45:25.549 --> 00:45:29.485
Wait a minute. Are you trying to tell us
he'd lie just to be important once?

675
00:45:29.553 --> 00:45:32.579
No. He wouldn't really lie.

676
00:45:32.656 --> 00:45:36.057
But perhaps he made himself believe
he heard those words...

677
00:45:36.126 --> 00:45:38.321
and recognized the boy's face.

678
00:45:38.395 --> 00:45:40.795
That's the most fantastic story
I've ever heard.

679
00:45:40.864 --> 00:45:43.765
How can you make up a thing like that?
What do you know about it?

680
00:45:53.143 --> 00:45:54.974
Does anybody want a cough drop?

681
00:45:55.045 --> 00:45:57.377
No, thanks.
- I'll take one.

682
00:45:57.447 --> 00:46:01.008
Say what you like, I don't see
how anybody can think he's not guilty.

683
00:46:01.084 --> 00:46:05.145
There's something else I'd like to talk
about for a minute. Thanks.

684
00:46:05.222 --> 00:46:08.487
I think we've proved that the old man
couldn't have heard the boy say it.

685
00:46:08.558 --> 00:46:10.685
But...
- You didn't prove it at all!

686
00:46:10.761 --> 00:46:13.093
Supposing he really did hear it.

687
00:46:13.163 --> 00:46:15.631
This phrase, how many times have
all of us used it?

688
00:46:15.699 --> 00:46:17.132
Probably thousands.

689
00:46:17.200 --> 00:46:19.168
''I could kill you for that,
darling.''

690
00:46:19.236 --> 00:46:22.228
''Junior, you do that once more,
and I'm gonna kill you.''

691
00:46:22.305 --> 00:46:24.637
We say it every day. That doesn't mean
we're really gonna kill anybody.

692
00:46:24.708 --> 00:46:27.734
What are you trying to give us here?
The phrase was, ''I'm going to kill you.''

693
00:46:27.811 --> 00:46:31.269
The kid yelled it at the top of his
lungs. Don't tell me he didn't mean it.

694
00:46:31.348 --> 00:46:35.148
Anybody says it like that, means it.
- Gee, I don't know.

695
00:46:35.218 --> 00:46:40.019
I was arguing with a guy I work with
at the bank a couple of weeks ago.

696
00:46:40.090 --> 00:46:42.024
He called me an idiot,
so I yelled at him.

697
00:46:42.092 --> 00:46:44.890
This guy's trying to make you
believe things that aren't so!

698
00:46:44.961 --> 00:46:47.020
The kid said it, and he killed him.

699
00:46:47.097 --> 00:46:52.000
Do you think the boy would shout that
so the whole neighborhood could hear?

700
00:46:52.068 --> 00:46:53.899
I don't think so.
He's much too bright.

701
00:46:53.970 --> 00:46:58.873
Bright? He's a common ignorant slob.
He don't even speak good English.

702
00:46:58.942 --> 00:47:02.469
He doesn't even speak good English.

703
00:47:04.214 --> 00:47:07.843
Mr. Foreman, I'd like to
change my vote to ''not guilty.''

704
00:47:07.918 --> 00:47:09.351
You what!

705
00:47:09.419 --> 00:47:12.877
You heard me.
- Are you sure?

706
00:47:12.956 --> 00:47:14.184
Yeah, I'm sure.

707
00:47:14.257 --> 00:47:16.987
The vote is nine to three
in favor of guilty.

708
00:47:17.060 --> 00:47:20.029
Well, if this isn't the living end.

709
00:47:23.700 --> 00:47:27.192
What are you basing it on?
Stories this guy made up?

710
00:47:27.270 --> 00:47:31.502
You ought to write for a detective
magazine. You'd make a fortune.

711
00:47:31.575 --> 00:47:35.204
The kid's own lawyer knew
he didn't stand a chance!

712
00:47:35.278 --> 00:47:38.770
Right from the beginning his own lawyer
knew. You could see it.

713
00:47:38.849 --> 00:47:43.183
Boy, oh, boy, this guy here
is really something.

714
00:47:43.253 --> 00:47:46.689
The kid had a lawyer, didn't he?
He presented his case, not you.

715
00:47:46.756 --> 00:47:49.725
How come you've got so much to say?
- Lawyers aren't infallible.

716
00:47:49.793 --> 00:47:52.660
Baltimore, please.
- He was court appointed.

717
00:47:52.729 --> 00:47:54.663
What's that supposed to mean?
- It could mean a lot of things.

718
00:47:54.731 --> 00:47:59.532
Maybe he didn't want the case
or he resented being appointed.

719
00:47:59.603 --> 00:48:03.061
No money, no glory, not even
much chance of winning.

720
00:48:03.139 --> 00:48:06.165
That's not a promising situation
for a young lawyer.

721
00:48:06.243 --> 00:48:07.767
He'd really have to believe in
his client...

722
00:48:07.844 --> 00:48:11.041
to put up a good case, and you
pointed out that he didn't.

723
00:48:11.114 --> 00:48:14.106
Of course he didn't.

724
00:48:14.184 --> 00:48:16.482
Who the heck could?

725
00:48:16.553 --> 00:48:19.454
Except maybe some guy's mother
or somebody.

726
00:48:19.522 --> 00:48:22.923
Look. Will you look at the time?
For crying out loud.

727
00:48:22.993 --> 00:48:27.157
Pardon me. I have some notes here.
- Notes.

728
00:48:27.230 --> 00:48:29.755
I would like, please, to say something.

729
00:48:29.833 --> 00:48:32.700
I have been listening very carefully.

730
00:48:32.769 --> 00:48:36.398
It seems to me that this man has some
very good points to make.

731
00:48:36.473 --> 00:48:41.137
From what was presented at the trial,
the boy looks guilty on the surface.

732
00:48:41.211 --> 00:48:45.272
But maybe if we go deeper?
- Come on, will ya?

733
00:48:45.348 --> 00:48:47.646
There's a question I would like to ask.

734
00:48:47.717 --> 00:48:51.118
Let us assume that the boy
really did commit the murder.

735
00:48:51.187 --> 00:48:54.645
This happened at ten minutes
after 12:00.

736
00:48:54.724 --> 00:48:57.124
How was he caught by the police?

737
00:48:57.193 --> 00:49:01.630
He came home at 3:00 or so...

738
00:49:01.698 --> 00:49:05.498
and he was captured by two detectives
in the hallway of his house.

739
00:49:05.568 --> 00:49:10.096
My question is, if he really had killed
his father...

740
00:49:10.173 --> 00:49:13.506
why would he come back home
three hours later?

741
00:49:13.576 --> 00:49:16.443
Wouldn't he be afraid of being caught?

742
00:49:16.513 --> 00:49:20.950
He came home to get his knife. It's not
nice to leave knives in people's chests.

743
00:49:21.017 --> 00:49:23.645
Yeah, especially relatives.

744
00:49:23.720 --> 00:49:25.813
I don't see anything funny about it.

745
00:49:25.889 --> 00:49:29.655
The boy knew the knife could be
identified as the one he just bought.

746
00:49:29.726 --> 00:49:31.819
He had to get it
before the police did.

747
00:49:31.895 --> 00:49:36.855
But if the knife could be identified,
why did he leave it in the first place?

748
00:49:36.933 --> 00:49:39.868
I think we can assume the boy ran out
in a state of panic...

749
00:49:39.936 --> 00:49:42.461
after having just killed his father.

750
00:49:42.539 --> 00:49:45.201
When he finally calmed down,
he realized he left his knife there.

751
00:49:45.275 --> 00:49:48.574
This then depends on your definition
of ''panic.''

752
00:49:48.645 --> 00:49:53.412
He was calm enough to see that there
were no fingerprints left on the knife.

753
00:49:53.483 --> 00:49:57.613
Now, where did this panic start
and where did it end?

754
00:49:57.687 --> 00:49:59.917
You voted ''guilty.''
What side are you on?

755
00:49:59.990 --> 00:50:03.721
I don't believe I have to
be loyal to one side.

756
00:50:03.793 --> 00:50:05.920
I am simply asking questions.

757
00:50:05.996 --> 00:50:08.988
This is just off the top of my head,
but...

758
00:50:09.065 --> 00:50:10.999
if I were the boy and I'd done
the stabbing...

759
00:50:11.067 --> 00:50:13.331
I'd take a chance
and go back for the knife.

760
00:50:13.403 --> 00:50:15.997
I'll bet he figured that nobody'd
seen him running out...

761
00:50:16.072 --> 00:50:18.666
and that the body wouldn't be
discovered till the next day.

762
00:50:18.742 --> 00:50:20.573
It was the middle of the night.

763
00:50:20.643 --> 00:50:23.111
I'll bet he figured nobody
would find the body till the next day.

764
00:50:23.179 --> 00:50:25.374
Pardon. This is my whole point.

765
00:50:25.448 --> 00:50:29.145
The woman testified that the moment
after she saw the killing...

766
00:50:29.219 --> 00:50:31.687
that is, a moment after the train
went by...

767
00:50:31.755 --> 00:50:34.952
she screamed and then went to telephone
the police.

768
00:50:35.025 --> 00:50:38.517
Now, the boy certainly must have heard
the scream...

769
00:50:38.595 --> 00:50:42.031
so he knew that somebody saw something.

770
00:50:42.098 --> 00:50:44.760
I just don't think
that he would have gone back.

771
00:50:44.834 --> 00:50:47.894
Two things. One. In his state of panic,
he may not have heard the scream.

772
00:50:47.971 --> 00:50:50.963
Perhaps it wasn't very loud.
Two. If he did hear it...

773
00:50:51.041 --> 00:50:52.975
he may not have connected it
with his own act.

774
00:50:53.043 --> 00:50:55.841
Remember, he lived in a neighborhood
where screams were fairly commonplace.

775
00:50:55.912 --> 00:50:57.140
There's your answer.

776
00:50:57.213 --> 00:51:00.649
Maybe. Maybe the boy
did kill his father.

777
00:51:00.717 --> 00:51:03.015
Didn't hear the scream,
did run out in a panic...

778
00:51:03.086 --> 00:51:08.023
did calm down and come back
for the knife, risking being caught.

779
00:51:08.091 --> 00:51:11.151
Maybe all those things happened,
but maybe they didn't!

780
00:51:11.227 --> 00:51:15.459
There's enough doubt that we can wonder
whether he was there during the killing.

781
00:51:15.532 --> 00:51:18.865
What are you talking about?
Didn't the old man see him running out?

782
00:51:18.935 --> 00:51:21.028
He's twisting the facts!

783
00:51:21.104 --> 00:51:24.540
Did or didn't the old man see the kid
running out of the house at 12:10?

784
00:51:24.607 --> 00:51:26.541
Did or didn't he?

785
00:51:26.609 --> 00:51:30.136
He says he did.
- Says? How do you like that!

786
00:51:30.213 --> 00:51:34.172
Witnesses can make mistakes.
- Sure, when you want them to, they do.

787
00:51:34.250 --> 00:51:38.880
Keep the yelling down!
- Maybe we need a little yelling.

788
00:51:38.955 --> 00:51:42.618
These guys are going off every
which way. Didn't hear the scream!

789
00:51:42.692 --> 00:51:46.321
What difference does it make?
You're talking about the little details.

790
00:51:46.396 --> 00:51:49.593
You're forgetting the important stuff.
- I'd like to call for another vote.

791
00:51:49.666 --> 00:51:53.124
I'm talking here!
- There's another vote called for.

792
00:51:53.203 --> 00:51:55.137
Now, let's take our seats.

793
00:51:58.741 --> 00:52:01.232
I never saw so much time spent
on nothing.

794
00:52:01.311 --> 00:52:04.542
It only takes a second.

795
00:52:04.614 --> 00:52:07.640
Okay. I guess the fastest way is to find
out who's voting ''not guilty.''

796
00:52:07.717 --> 00:52:10.379
All those voting ''not guilty,''
please raise your hands.

797
00:52:11.521 --> 00:52:15.252
Still the same. One, two,
three ''not guilty.'' Nine ''guilty.''

798
00:52:15.325 --> 00:52:20.353
Where are we? We can yakety-yak till
next Tuesday. Where's it getting us?

799
00:52:20.430 --> 00:52:24.389
Pardon. I vote ''not guilty.''

800
00:52:26.369 --> 00:52:29.736
We're all going crazy in here
or something.

801
00:52:29.806 --> 00:52:33.708
The kid is guilty.
Why don't you listen to the facts!

802
00:52:33.776 --> 00:52:35.937
Tell him, will ya?
This is getting to be a joke.

803
00:52:36.012 --> 00:52:39.140
The vote is eight to four
in favor of ''guilty.''

804
00:52:39.215 --> 00:52:43.276
What is this, love your underprivileged
brother week or something?

805
00:52:43.353 --> 00:52:47.119
I want you to get up and tell me why you
changed your vote. Give me reasons.

806
00:52:47.190 --> 00:52:51.786
I don't have to defend my decision
to you. I have a reasonable doubt.

807
00:52:51.861 --> 00:52:54.921
What reasonable doubt?
That's nothing but words. Look at this.

808
00:52:54.998 --> 00:52:59.935
The kid you decided isn't guilty
was seen ramming this into his father!

809
00:53:00.003 --> 00:53:01.994
Now, what about this,
Mr. Reasonable Doubt?

810
00:53:02.071 --> 00:53:04.733
That's not the knife.
Don't you remember?

811
00:53:04.807 --> 00:53:07.207
Oh, brilliant.

812
00:53:07.277 --> 00:53:11.577
This is the craziest.
What're we supposed to believe?

813
00:53:11.648 --> 00:53:14.446
You're sitting here. You're pulling
stories out of thin air.

814
00:53:14.584 --> 00:53:18.418
A guy like this, if he's sitting
ringside at the Dempsey fight...

815
00:53:18.488 --> 00:53:20.888
he'd be trying to tell you that...

816
00:53:20.957 --> 00:53:23.357
Look. What about the old man?

817
00:53:23.426 --> 00:53:25.621
Are we supposed to believe that he
didn't get up and run to his door...

818
00:53:25.695 --> 00:53:28.960
and see the kid 15 seconds
after the killing?

819
00:53:29.032 --> 00:53:31.933
He's just saying so to be important?
Right?

820
00:53:32.001 --> 00:53:34.333
What's the point of the whole thing?
- Hold it.

821
00:53:34.404 --> 00:53:36.497
And the Baltimore rooter
is heard from again.

822
00:53:36.573 --> 00:53:38.871
Pop-ups are falling for base hits...

823
00:53:38.942 --> 00:53:42.241
Now, look. Did the old man say
he ran to the door?

824
00:53:42.312 --> 00:53:45.543
Ran, walked, what's the difference?
He got there, didn't he?

825
00:53:45.615 --> 00:53:48.015
Wait...
- He said he ran. I think he did.

826
00:53:48.084 --> 00:53:51.315
I don't remember. But I don't see how
he could have run to the door.

827
00:53:51.387 --> 00:53:54.845
He said he went from his bedroom
to the front door. Isn't that enough?

828
00:53:54.924 --> 00:53:56.391
Where was the bedroom?
- Down the hall.

829
00:53:56.459 --> 00:53:58.950
I thought you remembered everything.
Don't you remember that?

830
00:53:59.028 --> 00:54:02.691
No. Mr. Foreman, I'd like to see
a diagram of the apartment.

831
00:54:02.765 --> 00:54:06.166
Why don't we run the trial over again?

832
00:54:06.236 --> 00:54:08.466
How come you're the only one
that wants to see exhibits?

833
00:54:08.538 --> 00:54:11.132
I want to see this one too.
- I'd like to stop wasting time.

834
00:54:11.207 --> 00:54:14.904
If we're gonna start wading through
where the body was...

835
00:54:14.978 --> 00:54:17.606
Not unless somebody else wants to.
But I'd like to know if an old man...

836
00:54:17.680 --> 00:54:20.376
who drags one foot
because of a stroke last year...

837
00:54:20.450 --> 00:54:23.044
can get from his bedroom
to his front door in 15 seconds.

838
00:54:23.119 --> 00:54:24.848
Twenty.
- He said fifteen.

839
00:54:24.921 --> 00:54:28.357
He said 20 seconds.
- He said 15.

840
00:54:28.424 --> 00:54:31.882
How does he know how long 15 seconds is?
You can't judge a thing like that!

841
00:54:31.961 --> 00:54:35.419
He said 15. He was very positive.
- He was an old man.

842
00:54:35.498 --> 00:54:39.195
Half the time he was confused.
How could he be positive about anything?

843
00:54:46.766 --> 00:54:50.862
I don't see what you're going to prove.
The man said he saw the boy running out.

844
00:54:50.937 --> 00:54:53.132
Let's see if the details
bear him out.

845
00:54:53.206 --> 00:54:56.232
As soon as the body hit the floor,
he said he heard footsteps upstairs...

846
00:54:56.309 --> 00:54:57.742
running toward the front door.

847
00:54:57.811 --> 00:55:01.042
Heard the upstairs door open,
the footsteps start down the stairs.

848
00:55:01.114 --> 00:55:02.979
He said he got to his own front door
as fast as he could...

849
00:55:03.049 --> 00:55:05.517
and he swore it couldn't
have been more than 15 seconds.

850
00:55:05.585 --> 00:55:07.280
If the killer
began running immediately...

851
00:55:07.354 --> 00:55:09.618
Maybe he didn't.
- The old man said he did.

852
00:55:09.689 --> 00:55:12.681
You ought to be in Atlantic City
at that hairsplitters' convention.

853
00:55:12.759 --> 00:55:15.455
Why don't you stop
making smart remarks all the time?

854
00:55:15.528 --> 00:55:18.929
For your three dollars a day,
you have to listen to everything.

855
00:55:18.999 --> 00:55:22.332
What about it?
- All right. Here's the apartment.

856
00:55:22.402 --> 00:55:26.065
The old man's apartment was
directly beneath and exactly the same.

857
00:55:26.139 --> 00:55:29.438
Here are the El tracks, the bedroom,
living room, kitchen, bathroom.

858
00:55:29.509 --> 00:55:31.568
Here's the hall.
Here are the stairs.

859
00:55:31.645 --> 00:55:33.772
The old man was in this bedroom
right here.

860
00:55:33.847 --> 00:55:36.441
He says he crossed to the door
and walked down the hall...

861
00:55:36.516 --> 00:55:39.508
opened the front door just in time
to see the boy running down the stairs.

862
00:55:39.586 --> 00:55:42.919
Am I right so far?
- That's the story, for the 19th time.

863
00:55:42.989 --> 00:55:45.617
Fifteen seconds after
the body hit the floor.

864
00:55:45.692 --> 00:55:48.820
Correct.
- Now, let's see.

865
00:55:48.895 --> 00:55:52.922
It's 12 feet from the bed to the door.
The hall is 43 feet.

866
00:55:52.999 --> 00:55:56.162
He would've had to walk 12 feet,
open the bedroom door...

867
00:55:56.236 --> 00:55:59.763
walk 43 feet down and
open the front door, all in 15 seconds.

868
00:55:59.839 --> 00:56:02.205
Do you think he could've done it?
- Sure, he could've done it.

869
00:56:02.275 --> 00:56:05.176
He can walk only very slowly. They had
to help him into the witness chair.

870
00:56:05.245 --> 00:56:07.042
You make it sound like a long walk.

871
00:56:07.113 --> 00:56:09.308
For an old man who had a stroke,
it is a long walk.

872
00:56:09.382 --> 00:56:11.475
What are you doing?

873
00:56:11.551 --> 00:56:14.452
I'm going to try it.
- What do you mean, try it?

874
00:56:14.521 --> 00:56:17.649
Why didn't his lawyer bring it up?
- Maybe he just didn't think about it.

875
00:56:17.724 --> 00:56:20.716
Didn't think of it? Do you think
the man's an idiot? It's obvious.

876
00:56:20.794 --> 00:56:23.228
Did you think of it?
- It don't matter.

877
00:56:23.296 --> 00:56:24.923
He didn't bring it up
because he knew it would hurt his case.

878
00:56:24.998 --> 00:56:27.296
What do you think of that?
- Maybe he didn't bring it up...

879
00:56:27.367 --> 00:56:29.927
'cause it would've meant bullying
and badgering a helpless old man.

880
00:56:30.003 --> 00:56:33.166
You know that doesn't sit well
with a jury. Most lawyers avoid it.

881
00:56:33.239 --> 00:56:36.072
So what kind of bum is he, then?
- That's what I've been asking.

882
00:56:36.142 --> 00:56:39.134
Pass me that chair, will you?
Those two chairs are the old man's bed.

883
00:56:39.212 --> 00:56:42.113
I just paced off 12 feet across
the room. This'll be the bedroom door.

884
00:56:42.182 --> 00:56:45.015
That's crazy.
You can't recreate a thing like that.

885
00:56:45.085 --> 00:56:48.486
The hall was 43 feet.
I'll pace to that wall and back again.

886
00:56:50.590 --> 00:56:54.287
Look. This is absolutely insane.
What do you waste everybody's time for?

887
00:56:54.361 --> 00:56:57.387
According to you, it will only
take 15 seconds. We can spare that.

888
00:57:00.333 --> 00:57:02.665
Come on. Knock it off.

889
00:57:05.071 --> 00:57:07.631
Okay, killer. Yeah.

890
00:57:10.643 --> 00:57:12.611
Will you stand right there
and mark the front door?

891
00:57:12.679 --> 00:57:15.341
It was chain-locked
according to the testimony, remember?

892
00:57:15.415 --> 00:57:17.474
Anybody got a watch with a second hand?
- I have.

893
00:57:17.550 --> 00:57:19.745
You want me to start, stamp your foot.
That'll be the body falling.

894
00:57:19.819 --> 00:57:22.049
You can time me from there.

895
00:57:22.122 --> 00:57:24.215
What are we going to do,
play Charades now?

896
00:57:24.290 --> 00:57:25.985
What are we waiting for?

897
00:57:26.059 --> 00:57:28.687
I want to wait
till the second hand reaches 60.

898
00:57:28.762 --> 00:57:30.354
Come on.

899
00:57:37.103 --> 00:57:40.231
Come on. Speed it up.
He walked twice as fast as that.

900
00:57:41.641 --> 00:57:44.007
You want me to walk faster
than that, I will.

901
00:58:00.927 --> 00:58:04.693
- Lock. Door. Stop.
- Right.

902
00:58:04.764 --> 00:58:06.288
What's the time?

903
00:58:06.366 --> 00:58:09.392
Exactly 41 seconds.

904
00:58:09.469 --> 00:58:11.869
Here's what I think happened:
The old man heard the fight...

905
00:58:11.938 --> 00:58:13.997
between the boy and his father
a few hours earlier.

906
00:58:14.073 --> 00:58:16.906
When lying in bed, he heard a body
hit the floor in the boy's apartment...

907
00:58:16.976 --> 00:58:20.537
heard the scream across the street, got
to his front door as fast as he could...

908
00:58:20.613 --> 00:58:23.081
heard somebody racing down the stairs
and assumed it was the boy.

909
00:58:23.149 --> 00:58:25.014
I think that's possible.
- ''Assumed''?

910
00:58:27.754 --> 00:58:30.621
Brother, I've seen all kinds
of dishonesty in my day...

911
00:58:30.690 --> 00:58:33.818
but this little display
takes the cake.

912
00:58:33.893 --> 00:58:36.418
You all come in here with your hearts
bleeding all over the floor...

913
00:58:36.496 --> 00:58:38.726
about slum kids and injustice.

914
00:58:38.798 --> 00:58:43.098
You listen to some fairy tales, start
getting through to these old ladies.

915
00:58:43.169 --> 00:58:46.070
Well, you're not getting through to me.
I've had enough.

916
00:58:46.139 --> 00:58:50.872
What's the matter with you guys? You
all know he's guilty! He's got to burn!

917
00:58:50.944 --> 00:58:52.673
You're letting him slip
through our fingers!

918
00:58:52.745 --> 00:58:55.077
''Slip through our fingers''?
Are you his executioner?

919
00:58:55.148 --> 00:58:58.015
I'm one of'em!

920
00:58:58.084 --> 00:59:01.884
Perhaps you'd like to pull the switch.
- For this kid, you bet I would.

921
00:59:01.955 --> 00:59:05.220
I feel sorry for you. What it must
feel like to want to pull the switch.

922
00:59:06.159 --> 00:59:10.152
Ever since you walked in, you've been
acting like a self-appointed avenger.

923
00:59:10.230 --> 00:59:13.222
You want to see this boy die because
you want it, not because of the facts.

924
00:59:14.467 --> 00:59:16.662
You're a sadist.

925
00:59:17.504 --> 00:59:20.564
Let go. I'll kill him.
I'll kill him!

926
00:59:20.640 --> 00:59:23.074
You don't really mean
you'll kill me, do you?

927
00:59:30.517 --> 00:59:32.815
Is there anything wrong, gentlemen?
I heard some noise.

928
00:59:32.886 --> 00:59:36.049
Oh, no. Everything's all right.
Friendly little argument.

929
00:59:37.423 --> 00:59:40.449
We're through with that diagram.
You can take it if you want.

930
00:59:48.868 --> 00:59:50.802
What are you looking at?

931
01:00:11.758 --> 01:00:14.591
I suppose somebody has to start off.
- I beg pardon.

932
01:00:14.661 --> 01:00:17.630
''I beg pardon.''
What are you so polite about?

933
01:00:17.697 --> 01:00:19.790
For the same reason you're not.

934
01:00:19.866 --> 01:00:21.993
It's the way I was brought up.

935
01:00:23.836 --> 01:00:27.795
This fighting...
That's not why we are here, to fight.

936
01:00:27.874 --> 01:00:31.401
We have a responsibility.

937
01:00:31.477 --> 01:00:35.971
This I have always thought
is a remarkable thing about democracy...

938
01:00:36.049 --> 01:00:37.983
that we are...

939
01:00:39.752 --> 01:00:42.915
what is the word...
''notified.''

940
01:00:42.989 --> 01:00:46.186
That we are notified by mail
to come down to this place...

941
01:00:46.259 --> 01:00:51.162
to decide on the guilt or innocence
of a man we...

942
01:00:51.230 --> 01:00:53.528
we have never heard of before.

943
01:00:53.600 --> 01:00:58.936
We have nothing to gain or lose
by our verdict.

944
01:00:59.005 --> 01:01:02.406
This is one of the reasons
why we are strong.

945
01:01:03.543 --> 01:01:06.512
We should not
make it a personal thing.

946
01:01:07.680 --> 01:01:09.307
Thank you.

947
01:01:10.984 --> 01:01:12.815
If nobody else has an idea,
I might have a cutie.

948
01:01:12.885 --> 01:01:15.649
I haven't given it much thought,
but let's throw it out on the stoop...

949
01:01:15.722 --> 01:01:17.212
see if the cat licks it up.

950
01:01:17.290 --> 01:01:19.656
''The cat licks it up''?
- Yeah.

951
01:01:24.330 --> 01:01:26.298
Look how dark
it's gettin' out there.

952
01:01:26.366 --> 01:01:28.527
I bet we're going
to have a storm now.

953
01:01:30.670 --> 01:01:33.161
Boy, oh, boy.
It's really hot, huh?

954
01:01:34.674 --> 01:01:37.643
Pardon me,
but don't you ever sweat?

955
01:01:38.911 --> 01:01:40.503
No, I don't.

956
01:01:41.280 --> 01:01:43.305
Listen, I...

957
01:01:43.383 --> 01:01:48.685
I was wondering if
maybe we should take another vote.

958
01:01:48.755 --> 01:01:53.215
Oh, great. Maybe we can follow this up
with a little dancing and refreshments.

959
01:01:54.994 --> 01:01:57.462
Mr. Foreman?
- That's okay with me.

960
01:01:57.530 --> 01:02:00.158
Anyone doesn't want to vote?

961
01:02:00.233 --> 01:02:02.758
Come on.
- I'm sorry.

962
01:02:02.835 --> 01:02:06.362
I think we ought to have an open ballot,
call out our votes.

963
01:02:06.439 --> 01:02:08.634
Let's see who stands where.

964
01:02:08.708 --> 01:02:11.472
That sounds fair to me.
Anyone object?

965
01:02:13.913 --> 01:02:16.609
I'll call off
your jury numbers. One.

966
01:02:17.684 --> 01:02:20.744
Oh, that's me.
I vote ''guilty.''

967
01:02:20.820 --> 01:02:23.755
Two?
- Not guilty.

968
01:02:24.524 --> 01:02:28.153
Number three?
- Guilty.

969
01:02:28.227 --> 01:02:30.161
Number four?

970
01:02:31.564 --> 01:02:33.191
Guilty.

971
01:02:33.266 --> 01:02:36.030
Number five?
- Not guilty.

972
01:02:37.203 --> 01:02:40.229
Number six?
- Not guilty.

973
01:02:41.307 --> 01:02:42.899
Number seven?

974
01:02:42.975 --> 01:02:44.909
Guilty.

975
01:02:44.977 --> 01:02:47.912
Number eight?
- Not guilty.

976
01:02:50.116 --> 01:02:52.983
Number nine?
- Not guilty.

977
01:02:53.052 --> 01:02:55.577
Number ten?
- Guilty.

978
01:02:56.989 --> 01:03:01.085
Number 11?
- Not guilty.

979
01:03:01.160 --> 01:03:02.354
Number 12?

980
01:03:05.898 --> 01:03:07.195
Guilty.

981
01:03:13.439 --> 01:03:15.270
The vote is now six to six.

982
01:03:16.442 --> 01:03:19.070
And we go
into extra innings here, huh?

983
01:03:20.713 --> 01:03:23.045
Six to six.
I'm telling you.

984
01:03:23.116 --> 01:03:27.416
Some of you people in here must
be out of your minds. A kid like that.

985
01:03:27.487 --> 01:03:30.854
I don't think the kind of boy he is
has anything to do with it.

986
01:03:30.923 --> 01:03:32.914
The facts are supposed
to determine the case.

987
01:03:32.992 --> 01:03:37.019
I'm sick and tired of facts.
You can twist them any way you like.

988
01:03:37.096 --> 01:03:40.588
That's exactly the point
this gentleman has been making.

989
01:03:40.666 --> 01:03:43.464
You keep shouting
at the top of your lungs.

990
01:03:44.637 --> 01:03:48.471
I'd like to be a few years younger.
That man gets on my...

991
01:03:52.078 --> 01:03:54.740
It's hot in here, isn't it?
- Want a drink of water?

992
01:03:54.814 --> 01:03:56.577
No, thanks.

993
01:04:06.425 --> 01:04:10.054
It's going to rain.
- How'd you figure that out, killer?

994
01:04:11.864 --> 01:04:16.528
And how come you changed your vote?
- It seemed there was room for doubt.

995
01:04:16.602 --> 01:04:19.628
You haven't got a leg to stand on.
- I don't feel that way.

996
01:04:19.705 --> 01:04:21.832
There were a lot
of details that never came out.

997
01:04:21.908 --> 01:04:25.275
Good luck.
- Come on. You're like everybody else.

998
01:04:25.344 --> 01:04:27.574
You think too much.
You get mixed up.

999
01:04:28.915 --> 01:04:32.908
Know what I mean?
- I don't think you have any right to...

1000
01:04:32.985 --> 01:04:34.418
Loudmouth.

1001
01:05:28.174 --> 01:05:30.734
Look at that come down,
will ya?

1002
01:05:31.878 --> 01:05:35.837
You know, this reminds me
of a storm we had last...

1003
01:05:37.984 --> 01:05:41.579
What was that? November?
I don't know. Something.

1004
01:05:41.654 --> 01:05:44.521
It was quite a storm we had there.
It was right in the middle of a game.

1005
01:05:45.825 --> 01:05:47.918
We were behind seven to six...

1006
01:05:47.994 --> 01:05:50.827
but we just started to move the ball,
a real tackle.

1007
01:05:50.897 --> 01:05:55.391
A cut here. Slash here.
A slash right there. And cut it there.

1008
01:05:55.468 --> 01:05:56.901
And...

1009
01:05:58.204 --> 01:06:00.672
we had this kid, Slattery.

1010
01:06:01.741 --> 01:06:03.868
He was an ox, a real ox.

1011
01:06:05.011 --> 01:06:07.571
I wish I had another one
just like him.

1012
01:06:07.647 --> 01:06:10.445
I forgot to tell you. I'm
the assistant head coach...

1013
01:06:10.516 --> 01:06:12.575
at the Andrew J. McCorkle
High School.

1014
01:06:12.652 --> 01:06:14.313
That's out in Queens.

1015
01:06:14.387 --> 01:06:16.218
And anyway...

1016
01:06:17.290 --> 01:06:19.588
we were starting
to move along real nice.

1017
01:06:19.659 --> 01:06:23.823
That whole line
is just coming apart.

1018
01:06:23.896 --> 01:06:27.662
And it starts to come down
cats and dogs just like this.

1019
01:06:27.733 --> 01:06:30.258
Just... whoosh... right down.

1020
01:06:31.637 --> 01:06:35.038
Well, it was murder,
you know?

1021
01:06:35.107 --> 01:06:37.541
I swear, I nearly bawled.

1022
01:06:37.610 --> 01:06:39.635
Just, you couldn't go nowhere.

1023
01:06:58.397 --> 01:07:00.831
What is it with this fan here?
How come...

1024
01:07:11.777 --> 01:07:15.008
Must have been on the same switch
with the lights.

1025
01:07:15.081 --> 01:07:17.015
Things are looking up here, huh?

1026
01:07:18.517 --> 01:07:19.950
Yeah.

1027
01:07:24.824 --> 01:07:26.758
Boy. Better.

1028
01:07:35.001 --> 01:07:36.935
Two points.

1029
01:07:40.906 --> 01:07:43.636
You guys ever go to the Garden?

1030
01:07:45.111 --> 01:07:48.103
That's a damn stupid thing to do.

1031
01:07:48.180 --> 01:07:52.480
I'm sorry. I... You know?

1032
01:07:54.453 --> 01:07:55.943
Pardon me.

1033
01:07:57.023 --> 01:07:59.457
How do you like it?
Even Steven.

1034
01:07:59.525 --> 01:08:01.823
Pretty surprising, isn't it?

1035
01:08:01.894 --> 01:08:03.327
Yes.

1036
01:08:05.164 --> 01:08:08.099
Say, that business before,
when that tall guy...

1037
01:08:08.167 --> 01:08:10.260
what's his name...
was trying to bait me.

1038
01:08:10.336 --> 01:08:14.102
That doesn't prove anything.
I'm a pretty excitable person.

1039
01:08:14.173 --> 01:08:18.075
Where does he come off calling me
a public avenger, sadist and everything?

1040
01:08:18.144 --> 01:08:20.635
Anyone in his right mind
would blow his stack.

1041
01:08:21.847 --> 01:08:24.111
He was just trying to bait me.

1042
01:08:24.183 --> 01:08:26.083
He did an excellent job.

1043
01:08:28.854 --> 01:08:31.049
I'll tell you what I think.
We're going nowhere here.

1044
01:08:31.123 --> 01:08:33.591
I'm ready to walk into court right now
and declare a hung jury.

1045
01:08:33.659 --> 01:08:37.095
No point in this going on anymore.
- I go for that too.

1046
01:08:37.163 --> 01:08:39.757
Let's take it into the judge and let the
kid take his chance with 12 other guys.

1047
01:08:39.832 --> 01:08:41.800
I don't think the judge
will accept a hung jury.

1048
01:08:41.867 --> 01:08:44.461
We haven't been in here very long.
- Well, let's find out.

1049
01:08:44.537 --> 01:08:46.596
I am not in favor of that.
- Listen.

1050
01:08:46.672 --> 01:08:49.800
This kid wouldn't stand a chance
with another jury, and you know it.

1051
01:08:49.875 --> 01:08:52.810
Come on. We're hung. Nobody's going to
change his vote. Let's take it inside.

1052
01:08:52.878 --> 01:08:54.937
You still don't think
there's room for reasonable doubt?

1053
01:08:55.014 --> 01:08:57.676
No, I don't.
- Pardon.

1054
01:08:57.750 --> 01:09:00.878
Maybe you don't fully
understand the term ''reasonable doubt.''

1055
01:09:01.654 --> 01:09:03.679
What do you mean,
I don't understand?

1056
01:09:04.757 --> 01:09:06.884
Well, how do you like this guy?

1057
01:09:06.959 --> 01:09:09.928
I'm telling you. They're all alike.
They come here running for their life...

1058
01:09:09.995 --> 01:09:13.761
and before they can take a deep breath,
they're tellin' us how to run the show.

1059
01:09:13.833 --> 01:09:15.698
Boy, the arrogance of this guy.

1060
01:09:15.768 --> 01:09:20.467
All right. Let's stop the arguing
for about two minutes in here.

1061
01:09:20.539 --> 01:09:22.074
Who's got
something constructive to say?

1062
01:09:22.274 --> 01:09:22.831
Who's got
something constructive to say?

1063
01:09:22.908 --> 01:09:26.571
I'd like to go over something,
if you gentlemen don't mind.

1064
01:09:26.645 --> 01:09:31.378
An important point for the prosecution
was that after the boy claimed...

1065
01:09:31.450 --> 01:09:33.941
he'd been at the movies during the hours
the killing took place...

1066
01:09:34.019 --> 01:09:38.183
couldn't remember the names
of the movies or the stars in them...

1067
01:09:38.257 --> 01:09:40.885
This gentleman here has brought
that point in here several times.

1068
01:09:40.960 --> 01:09:44.157
That's correct.
It was the only alibi the boy offered.

1069
01:09:44.230 --> 01:09:46.858
He himself couldn't back it up
with any details at all.

1070
01:09:46.932 --> 01:09:50.005
Putting yourself in the boy's place, do
you think you could remember details...

1071
01:09:50.174 --> 01:09:53.610
after an experience such as being
slapped in the face by your father?

1072
01:09:53.678 --> 01:09:56.806
I think so, if there
were any special details to remember.

1073
01:09:56.881 --> 01:09:59.907
The boy couldn't remember the names
of the movies because he wasn't there.

1074
01:10:00.584 --> 01:10:02.984
According to the police testimony
in court, the boy was questioned...

1075
01:10:03.054 --> 01:10:05.386
by the detectives
in the kitchen of his apartment...

1076
01:10:05.456 --> 01:10:08.448
while the body of his father
was lying on the floor in the bedroom.

1077
01:10:08.526 --> 01:10:11.086
Do you think you could remember details
under those circumstances?

1078
01:10:11.162 --> 01:10:13.062
I do.
- Under great emotional stress?

1079
01:10:13.130 --> 01:10:15.621
Under great emotional stress.
- He remembered them correctly in court.

1080
01:10:15.700 --> 01:10:17.600
He named the pictures
and the stars who played in them.

1081
01:10:17.668 --> 01:10:21.126
Yes. His lawyer took great pains
to bring that out.

1082
01:10:21.205 --> 01:10:23.605
He had three months from the night
of the murder to the day of the trial...

1083
01:10:23.674 --> 01:10:25.301
in which to memorize them.

1084
01:10:25.376 --> 01:10:27.207
It's not difficult for a lawyer
to find out what played...

1085
01:10:27.278 --> 01:10:29.872
at a particular theater
on a particular night.

1086
01:10:29.947 --> 01:10:32.438
I'll take the testimony of the policeman
who interrogated the boy...

1087
01:10:32.516 --> 01:10:36.111
right after the murder, when he couldn't
remember a thing about the movies...

1088
01:10:36.187 --> 01:10:37.950
great emotional stress
or not.

1089
01:10:38.022 --> 01:10:40.889
I'd like
to ask you a personal question.

1090
01:10:40.958 --> 01:10:43.586
Go ahead.
- Where were you last night?

1091
01:10:43.661 --> 01:10:46.129
I was home.
- How about the night before that?

1092
01:10:46.197 --> 01:10:48.757
Come on. What is this?
- It's all right.

1093
01:10:48.833 --> 01:10:51.529
I left the office at 8:30,
went straight home and to bed.

1094
01:10:51.602 --> 01:10:57.006
And the night before that?
- Tuesday night bridge tournament.

1095
01:10:57.074 --> 01:10:58.939
I played bridge.
- Monday night?

1096
01:10:59.009 --> 01:11:02.376
When you get down to New Year's Eve,
1954, let me know, huh?

1097
01:11:04.482 --> 01:11:06.245
Monday night?

1098
01:11:07.618 --> 01:11:10.815
Monday night. My wife and I
went to the movies.

1099
01:11:10.888 --> 01:11:13.652
<i>What did you see?
- The Scarlet Circle. Clever whodunit.</i>

1100
01:11:13.724 --> 01:11:16.625
<i>What was the second feature?
- The...</i>

1101
01:11:18.529 --> 01:11:23.762
<i>I'll tell you in a minute.
The Remarkable Mrs...</i>

1102
01:11:24.835 --> 01:11:26.427
something...

1103
01:11:28.139 --> 01:11:30.573
<i>Mrs. Bainbridge.
The Remarkable Mrs. Bainbridge.</i>

1104
01:11:30.641 --> 01:11:33.371
<i>I saw that. It's called
The Amazing Mrs. Bainbridge.</i>

1105
01:11:34.879 --> 01:11:37.074
<i>Yes. The Amazing Mrs. Bainbridge.
I think that's right.</i>

1106
01:11:37.148 --> 01:11:39.776
<i>Who was
in The Amazing Mrs. Bainbridge?</i>

1107
01:11:39.850 --> 01:11:43.809
Barbara Long,
I think it was.

1108
01:11:43.888 --> 01:11:45.788
Dark. Very pretty girl.

1109
01:11:47.258 --> 01:11:50.750
''Ling'' or ''Long.'' Something like that.
- Who else?

1110
01:11:50.828 --> 01:11:54.229
I'd never heard of them before.
It was a second feature...

1111
01:11:54.298 --> 01:11:57.426
with unknown...

1112
01:11:57.501 --> 01:12:00.299
And you weren't
under an emotional stress, were you?

1113
01:12:05.609 --> 01:12:07.372
I wasn't.

1114
01:12:08.979 --> 01:12:12.415
I think the point is made.
- Big point.

1115
01:12:13.818 --> 01:12:15.809
You can talk till your tongue
is draggin' on the floor.

1116
01:12:15.886 --> 01:12:19.117
The boy is guilty, period.
Know what I mean, my friend?

1117
01:12:20.191 --> 01:12:24.287
Who's got those cough drops?
- They're all gone, my friend.

1118
01:12:25.896 --> 01:12:29.195
Boy. Look at that rain.
There goes your ball game.

1119
01:12:30.334 --> 01:12:32.802
It's only a shower.

1120
01:12:32.870 --> 01:12:35.361
Besides,
they got the infield covered.

1121
01:12:35.439 --> 01:12:37.964
Say, could I
see that knife a second, please?

1122
01:12:41.345 --> 01:12:43.370
We're still tied up six to six.

1123
01:12:43.447 --> 01:12:45.381
Who's got a suggestion?

1124
01:12:45.449 --> 01:12:47.417
It's five after 6:00.
Let's get some dinner.

1125
01:12:47.485 --> 01:12:49.919
Why don't we wait until 7:00,
give it another hour?

1126
01:12:49.987 --> 01:12:52.114
Okay with me.

1127
01:12:52.189 --> 01:12:54.350
There's something I'd like to say.

1128
01:12:54.425 --> 01:12:57.223
I mean, it's been bothering me
a little, and as long as we're stuck...

1129
01:12:58.295 --> 01:13:00.991
Well, there was this whole business
about the stab wound...

1130
01:13:01.065 --> 01:13:03.659
and how it was made,
the downward angle of it, you know?

1131
01:13:03.734 --> 01:13:05.793
Don't tell me we're going
to start with that again.

1132
01:13:05.870 --> 01:13:07.428
They've been over it
and over it.

1133
01:13:07.505 --> 01:13:09.996
I know they did,
but I don't go along with it.

1134
01:13:10.074 --> 01:13:12.565
Now, the boy was 5'7'' tall.

1135
01:13:12.643 --> 01:13:16.545
His father was 6'2''.
That's a difference of 7 inches.

1136
01:13:16.614 --> 01:13:20.948
It's very awkward to stab down into
the chest of someone taller than you.

1137
01:13:21.018 --> 01:13:23.418
Give me that.

1138
01:13:23.487 --> 01:13:26.115
I'll give you a demonstration.
Somebody get up.

1139
01:13:34.365 --> 01:13:36.629
I want you to watch this 'cause I don't
want to have to do it again.

1140
01:13:36.700 --> 01:13:39.533
I'll make myself
about six or seven inches shorter, okay?

1141
01:13:39.603 --> 01:13:42.572
It's about right. Maybe a little more.
- Okay. A little more.

1142
01:13:56.086 --> 01:13:59.453
Nobody's hurt, right?

1143
01:13:59.523 --> 01:14:01.923
Right. Nobody hurt.

1144
01:14:04.828 --> 01:14:09.731
This is the way I'd stab a man
who was taller than I was.

1145
01:14:09.800 --> 01:14:12.360
Look at the angle.
Down and in.

1146
01:14:12.436 --> 01:14:15.599
This is the way it was done.
Tell me I'm wrong.

1147
01:14:18.876 --> 01:14:23.313
I guess there's no argument.
- Hold it a minute. Give me that.

1148
01:14:25.182 --> 01:14:28.777
Boy, I hate these things.
Did you ever see a knife fight?

1149
01:14:28.852 --> 01:14:30.547
You?
- No.

1150
01:14:30.621 --> 01:14:33.488
Anybody here
ever see a knife fight?

1151
01:14:33.557 --> 01:14:36.321
Well, I have.
You know, my back stoop.

1152
01:14:36.393 --> 01:14:38.691
Lot across the street.
Backyard.

1153
01:14:38.762 --> 01:14:41.458
Switchblades came with the neighborhood
where I lived.

1154
01:14:41.532 --> 01:14:44.330
Funny. I never thought of it before.
I guess you try to forget those things.

1155
01:14:44.401 --> 01:14:47.893
How do you use a switchblade?
- Well, you'd never use it like this.

1156
01:14:49.640 --> 01:14:52.473
See? You'd lose too much time
switchin' hands.

1157
01:14:53.844 --> 01:14:56.335
Here's how: underhanded.

1158
01:14:59.049 --> 01:15:02.507
Anyone who's ever used a switch knife
wouldn't handle it any other way.

1159
01:15:02.586 --> 01:15:04.781
Are you sure?
- I'm sure.

1160
01:15:04.855 --> 01:15:06.686
That's why they're made
to open like that.

1161
01:15:06.757 --> 01:15:09.521
The boy was handy with a knife?
- Mm-hmm.

1162
01:15:09.593 --> 01:15:12.562
You think he could've made the kind
of wound that killed his father?

1163
01:15:12.630 --> 01:15:16.726
Not with the experience he'd had
all his life handling these things.

1164
01:15:16.800 --> 01:15:18.631
I feel he'd have
gone for him underhanded.

1165
01:15:18.702 --> 01:15:21.933
How do you know? Were you in the room
when the father was killed?

1166
01:15:22.006 --> 01:15:23.997
No. Neither was anybody else.

1167
01:15:24.074 --> 01:15:27.441
What are you giving us all this
mumbo jumbo for? I don't believe it.

1168
01:15:27.511 --> 01:15:29.911
I don't think you can determine
what type of wound the boy might...

1169
01:15:29.980 --> 01:15:33.347
or might not have made simply because
he knew how to handle a knife.

1170
01:15:33.417 --> 01:15:36.545
What do you think?
- I don't know.

1171
01:15:36.620 --> 01:15:39.316
What do you mean, you don't know?
- I don't know.

1172
01:15:44.061 --> 01:15:46.120
How about you?

1173
01:15:46.196 --> 01:15:49.825
I don't know about the rest of them, but
I'm getting tired of yakety-yakking.

1174
01:15:49.900 --> 01:15:53.392
It's getting us nowhere.
So I guess I'll have to break it up.

1175
01:15:53.470 --> 01:15:57.167
I change my vote to ''not guilty.''
- You what?

1176
01:15:57.241 --> 01:15:59.334
You heard me.
I've had enough.

1177
01:15:59.410 --> 01:16:01.401
What do you mean,
you've had enough? That's no answer.

1178
01:16:01.478 --> 01:16:04.777
Listen. You just
take care of yourself, huh?

1179
01:16:04.848 --> 01:16:07.146
You know?
- He's right.

1180
01:16:07.217 --> 01:16:09.208
That's not an answer.

1181
01:16:10.287 --> 01:16:13.154
What kind of a man are you?

1182
01:16:13.223 --> 01:16:16.386
You have sat here and voted ''guilty''
with everyone else...

1183
01:16:16.460 --> 01:16:20.692
because there are some baseball tickets
burning a hole in your pocket.

1184
01:16:20.764 --> 01:16:25.724
And now you have changed your vote
because you're sick of all the talking?

1185
01:16:25.803 --> 01:16:27.395
Listen, buddy...

1186
01:16:27.471 --> 01:16:32.135
Who tells you you have the right
to play like this with a man's life?

1187
01:16:32.209 --> 01:16:34.905
Don't you care...
- Wait a minute.

1188
01:16:34.978 --> 01:16:38.880
You can't talk like that to me.
- I can talk like that to you.

1189
01:16:38.949 --> 01:16:42.214
If you want to vote ''not guilty,''
then do it because you are convinced...

1190
01:16:42.286 --> 01:16:45.187
the man is not guilty,
not because you've had enough.

1191
01:16:45.255 --> 01:16:47.780
And if you think he is guilty,
then vote that way.

1192
01:16:47.858 --> 01:16:51.157
But don't you have the guts
to do what you think is right?

1193
01:16:51.228 --> 01:16:53.355
Now, listen...
- Guilty or not guilty?

1194
01:16:54.364 --> 01:16:57.731
I told you. Not guilty.
- Why?

1195
01:16:59.636 --> 01:17:02.799
Look, I don't have to...
- You do have to. Say it.

1196
01:17:02.873 --> 01:17:04.465
Why?

1197
01:17:04.541 --> 01:17:06.805
I don't think he's guilty.

1198
01:17:16.290 --> 01:17:20.556
I want another vote.
- That's another vote called for.

1199
01:17:20.627 --> 01:17:23.596
I guess the quickest way
is a show of hands. Anyone object?

1200
01:17:23.664 --> 01:17:26.394
All those voting ''not guilty''
raise your hands.

1201
01:17:27.301 --> 01:17:29.769
One, two, three...

1202
01:17:29.836 --> 01:17:32.430
four, five, six, seven...

1203
01:17:35.375 --> 01:17:36.808
eight...

1204
01:17:42.182 --> 01:17:43.615
nine.

1205
01:17:45.986 --> 01:17:49.114
All those voting ''guilty,''
raise your hands.

1206
01:17:49.189 --> 01:17:51.623
One, two, three.

1207
01:17:51.692 --> 01:17:54.957
The vote's nine to three
in favor of acquittal.

1208
01:17:55.028 --> 01:17:57.258
I don't understand you people.

1209
01:17:57.331 --> 01:18:01.062
All these picky little points you keep
bringing up. They don't mean nothing.

1210
01:18:01.134 --> 01:18:03.068
You saw this kid
just like I did.

1211
01:18:03.136 --> 01:18:05.070
You're not going to tell me
you believe that phony story...

1212
01:18:05.138 --> 01:18:08.699
about losing the knife and that business
about being at the movies.

1213
01:18:08.775 --> 01:18:11.767
You know how these people lie.
It's born in them.

1214
01:18:11.845 --> 01:18:13.904
What the heck!
I don't have to tell you.

1215
01:18:13.980 --> 01:18:15.948
They don't know
what the truth is.

1216
01:18:16.016 --> 01:18:19.679
And let me tell you, they don't need
any big reason to kill someone either.

1217
01:18:19.753 --> 01:18:22.620
No, sir.
They get drunk.

1218
01:18:22.689 --> 01:18:25.681
They're real big drinkers,
all of them. You know that.

1219
01:18:25.759 --> 01:18:28.023
And, bang,
someone's lying in the gutter.

1220
01:18:28.095 --> 01:18:31.087
Nobody's blaming them for it.
That's the way they are by nature.

1221
01:18:31.164 --> 01:18:33.394
You know what I mean?
Violent.

1222
01:18:33.467 --> 01:18:37.563
Where are you going? Human life don't
mean as much to them as it does to us.

1223
01:18:38.872 --> 01:18:41.670
They're lushing it up
and fighting all the time...

1224
01:18:41.742 --> 01:18:43.801
and if somebody gets killed,
so somebody gets killed.

1225
01:18:43.877 --> 01:18:47.608
They don't care. Sure, there's
some good things about 'em too.

1226
01:18:47.681 --> 01:18:51.139
I'm the first one to say that.
I've known a couple who were okay...

1227
01:18:51.218 --> 01:18:53.186
but that's the exception,
know what I mean?

1228
01:18:53.253 --> 01:18:57.019
Most of them, it's like they
have no feelings. They can do anything.

1229
01:18:57.090 --> 01:18:59.650
What's going on here?

1230
01:18:59.726 --> 01:19:03.787
I'm trying to tell you.
You're making a big mistake, you people.

1231
01:19:03.864 --> 01:19:06.799
This kid is a liar. I know it.
I know all about them.

1232
01:19:08.668 --> 01:19:10.727
Listen to me.
They're no good.

1233
01:19:10.804 --> 01:19:13.034
There's not a one of them
who's any good.

1234
01:19:15.308 --> 01:19:17.708
I mean, what's happening in here?

1235
01:19:17.778 --> 01:19:19.837
I'm speaking my piece,
and you...

1236
01:19:21.281 --> 01:19:23.374
Listen to me.

1237
01:19:25.452 --> 01:19:28.683
This kid on trial here...
his type...

1238
01:19:28.755 --> 01:19:30.689
don't you know about them?

1239
01:19:32.125 --> 01:19:34.320
There's a danger here.

1240
01:19:35.562 --> 01:19:37.723
These people are dangerous.

1241
01:19:37.798 --> 01:19:39.823
They're wild.

1242
01:19:40.901 --> 01:19:42.766
Listen to me.

1243
01:19:44.037 --> 01:19:48.371
I have. Now sit down
and don't open your mouth again.

1244
01:19:54.114 --> 01:19:55.706
I'm telling you.

1245
01:20:31.451 --> 01:20:35.717
It's always difficult to keep personal
prejudice out of a thing like this.

1246
01:20:36.857 --> 01:20:40.623
Wherever you run into it,
prejudice always obscures the truth.

1247
01:20:42.529 --> 01:20:44.929
I don't really know
what the truth is.

1248
01:20:44.998 --> 01:20:48.024
I don't suppose anybody
will ever really know.

1249
01:20:49.169 --> 01:20:53.105
Nine of us now seem to feel
that the defendant is innocent...

1250
01:20:53.173 --> 01:20:56.199
but we're just
gambling on probabilities.

1251
01:20:56.276 --> 01:20:58.244
We may be wrong.

1252
01:20:58.311 --> 01:21:02.042
We may be trying to
let a guilty man go free. I don't know.

1253
01:21:02.115 --> 01:21:04.310
Nobody really can.

1254
01:21:04.384 --> 01:21:06.716
But we have a reasonable doubt.

1255
01:21:06.786 --> 01:21:10.313
That's something
that's very valuable in our system.

1256
01:21:10.390 --> 01:21:14.918
No jury can declare a man guilty
unless it's sure.

1257
01:21:16.530 --> 01:21:21.593
We nine can't understand
how you three are still so sure.

1258
01:21:22.602 --> 01:21:24.968
Maybe you can tell us.
- I'll try.

1259
01:21:26.039 --> 01:21:29.668
You've made some excellent points,
but I still believe the boy is guilty.

1260
01:21:29.743 --> 01:21:34.578
I have two reasons. One: the evidence
given by the woman across the street...

1261
01:21:34.648 --> 01:21:37.310
who actually
saw the murder committed.

1262
01:21:37.384 --> 01:21:39.909
As far as I'm concerned,
that's the most important testimony.

1263
01:21:39.986 --> 01:21:42.614
And two: the fact
she described the stabbing...

1264
01:21:42.689 --> 01:21:47.183
by saying she saw the boy raise his arm
over his head and stab down...

1265
01:21:47.260 --> 01:21:48.921
into the father's chest.

1266
01:21:48.995 --> 01:21:52.897
She saw him do it the wrong way.
- That's absolutely right.

1267
01:21:53.567 --> 01:21:55.762
Let's talk about this woman
for a moment.

1268
01:21:55.835 --> 01:21:59.066
She said she went to bed
about 11:00 that night.

1269
01:21:59.139 --> 01:22:02.631
Her bed was next to the window, and
she could look out while lying down...

1270
01:22:02.709 --> 01:22:06.805
and see directly into the boy's room
across the street.

1271
01:22:06.880 --> 01:22:09.280
She tossed and turned for over an hour,
unable to sleep.

1272
01:22:09.349 --> 01:22:13.752
Finally, she turned toward the window
at about ten minutes after 12:00...

1273
01:22:13.820 --> 01:22:17.119
and as she looked out, she saw
the killing through the windows...

1274
01:22:17.190 --> 01:22:19.055
of a passing El train.

1275
01:22:19.125 --> 01:22:21.753
She said the lights went out
immediately after the killing...

1276
01:22:21.828 --> 01:22:27.562
but that she got a good look at the boy
in the act of stabbing his father.

1277
01:22:27.634 --> 01:22:32.298
As far as I can see it,
this is unshakable testimony.

1278
01:22:32.372 --> 01:22:34.636
That's the whole case.
- What do you think?

1279
01:22:39.980 --> 01:22:41.845
How about you?

1280
01:22:43.717 --> 01:22:46.208
I don't know.

1281
01:22:46.286 --> 01:22:49.949
So much evidence to sift.
This is pretty complicated business.

1282
01:22:50.023 --> 01:22:54.187
Frankly, I don't see
how you can vote for acquittal.

1283
01:22:54.260 --> 01:22:56.592
It's not so easy
to arrange all the evidence in order...

1284
01:22:56.663 --> 01:22:58.358
You can throw out
all the other evidence!

1285
01:22:58.431 --> 01:23:02.265
The woman saw him do it.
What else do you want?

1286
01:23:02.335 --> 01:23:04.269
Maybe...
- Let's vote on it.

1287
01:23:04.337 --> 01:23:08.398
There's another vote called for.
Anyone object?

1288
01:23:08.475 --> 01:23:11.308
All right. I'm changing my vote.
He's guilty.

1289
01:23:11.378 --> 01:23:14.074
Anybody else?
The vote is eight to four.

1290
01:23:14.147 --> 01:23:17.446
Why is this such a personal trial
for you? It's one vote.

1291
01:23:17.517 --> 01:23:21.317
I say we're a hung jury.
I say we take it into the judge.

1292
01:23:21.388 --> 01:23:24.186
How about it?
I want to hear arguments.

1293
01:23:24.257 --> 01:23:26.919
You're the leader of the cause.
What about it?

1294
01:23:28.828 --> 01:23:32.662
Let's go over it again.
- We've been over it again.

1295
01:23:32.732 --> 01:23:37.362
The boy here is bouncing backwards
and forwards like a tennis ball.

1296
01:23:37.437 --> 01:23:41.601
No point in getting nasty. You keep
trying to turn this into a contest.

1297
01:23:44.944 --> 01:23:47.811
Maybe we can talk about
setting some kind of time limit.

1298
01:23:47.881 --> 01:23:50.008
Yeah. Once around
for the dealer, huh?

1299
01:23:51.084 --> 01:23:54.815
It's a quarter after 6:00.

1300
01:23:54.888 --> 01:23:57.015
Someone before mentioned 7:00.

1301
01:23:57.090 --> 01:23:59.354
I think that's a point at which we
might begin to discuss the question...

1302
01:23:59.426 --> 01:24:01.485
of whether we're a hung jury
or not.

1303
01:24:02.829 --> 01:24:05.627
Don't you feel well?

1304
01:24:05.699 --> 01:24:07.667
I feel perfectly well,
thank you.

1305
01:24:07.734 --> 01:24:10.532
I was saying that 7:00
would be a reasonable time...

1306
01:24:10.603 --> 01:24:15.040
The reason I asked about that was
you were rubbing your nose like...

1307
01:24:15.108 --> 01:24:17.872
Oh, I'm sorry for interrupting...

1308
01:24:17.944 --> 01:24:21.209
but you made a gesture
that reminded me of something.

1309
01:24:21.281 --> 01:24:23.249
I'm trying to settle something here.
Do you mind?

1310
01:24:23.316 --> 01:24:26.114
I think this is important.

1311
01:24:26.720 --> 01:24:30.952
Thank you. Now, then,
I'm sure you'll pardon me for this...

1312
01:24:31.024 --> 01:24:34.118
but I was wondering
why you rub your nose like that.

1313
01:24:34.194 --> 01:24:35.593
Come on.

1314
01:24:35.662 --> 01:24:39.029
At this point, I happen to be talking
to the gentleman next to you.

1315
01:24:40.100 --> 01:24:43.035
Now, why were you
rubbing your nose like that?

1316
01:24:43.636 --> 01:24:47.402
Well, if it's any of your business,
I was rubbing it because it bothers me.

1317
01:24:47.474 --> 01:24:51.570
Oh, I'm sorry.
Is it because of your eyeglasses?

1318
01:24:51.644 --> 01:24:54.301
It is. Now, can we get on
to something else?

1319
01:24:54.936 --> 01:24:59.566
Your eyeglasses made those two deep
impressions on the sides of your nose.

1320
01:24:59.640 --> 01:25:03.371
I hadn't noticed that before.
That must be annoying.

1321
01:25:03.444 --> 01:25:05.435
It is very annoying.

1322
01:25:05.513 --> 01:25:11.281
I wouldn't know about that. I've
never worn eyeglasses. Twenty-twenty.

1323
01:25:11.352 --> 01:25:14.185
Will you come on already
with the optometrist bit?

1324
01:25:15.256 --> 01:25:18.885
The woman who testified
that she saw the killing...

1325
01:25:18.960 --> 01:25:23.158
had those same marks
on the sides of her nose.

1326
01:25:23.231 --> 01:25:25.426
Holy smokes.
You know, he's right.

1327
01:25:25.500 --> 01:25:28.628
Please just give me a minute...

1328
01:25:29.437 --> 01:25:31.268
and I'll be finished.

1329
01:25:31.339 --> 01:25:34.240
I don't know if anyone else
knows that about her.

1330
01:25:34.308 --> 01:25:39.075
I didn't think of it then,
but I've been going over her face...

1331
01:25:39.147 --> 01:25:41.342
in my mind.

1332
01:25:41.416 --> 01:25:45.512
She had those same marks.
She kept rubbing them in court.

1333
01:25:46.254 --> 01:25:48.313
He's right.
She did do that a lot.

1334
01:25:49.690 --> 01:25:53.387
This woman was about 45 years old.

1335
01:25:54.462 --> 01:25:58.398
She was making a tremendous effort
to look 35...

1336
01:25:58.466 --> 01:26:00.400
for her first public appearance.

1337
01:26:00.468 --> 01:26:03.995
Heavy makeup. Dyed hair.

1338
01:26:04.072 --> 01:26:07.974
Brand-new clothes that should
have been worn by a younger woman.

1339
01:26:08.042 --> 01:26:11.205
No glasses. Women do that.

1340
01:26:11.279 --> 01:26:14.180
See if you can
get the mental picture of her.

1341
01:26:14.248 --> 01:26:17.513
What do you mean, ''no glasses''? How
do you know whether she wore glasses?

1342
01:26:17.585 --> 01:26:20.713
Just 'cause she was rubbin' her nose?
- She had those marks. I saw 'em.

1343
01:26:20.788 --> 01:26:22.722
So what?
What do you think that means?

1344
01:26:22.790 --> 01:26:25.486
I'm getting sick of your yelling...
- Forget it.

1345
01:26:26.427 --> 01:26:30.761
Listen. He's right. I saw them too.
I was the closest one to her.

1346
01:26:30.832 --> 01:26:33.858
She had these things on the side
of her nose. What do you call those?

1347
01:26:33.935 --> 01:26:35.903
What point are you making here?

1348
01:26:35.970 --> 01:26:40.373
She had dyed hair, marks on her nose.
What does that mean?

1349
01:26:40.441 --> 01:26:44.172
Could those marks be made
by anything other than eyeglasses?

1350
01:26:47.782 --> 01:26:50.148
No, they couldn't.

1351
01:26:50.218 --> 01:26:52.778
I didn't see any marks!

1352
01:26:53.855 --> 01:26:57.916
I did. Strange,
but I didn't think about it before.

1353
01:26:57.992 --> 01:27:00.119
What about the lawyer?
Why didn't he say something?

1354
01:27:00.761 --> 01:27:03.423
There are 12 people in here
concentrating on this case.

1355
01:27:03.498 --> 01:27:06.092
Eleven of us
didn't think of it either.

1356
01:27:06.167 --> 01:27:08.692
What about the district attorney?
You think he'd pull a trick like that?

1357
01:27:08.769 --> 01:27:11.602
Have her testify without her glasses?
- Did you ever see a woman...

1358
01:27:11.672 --> 01:27:14.539
who had to wear glasses and didn't
want to because they spoil her looks?

1359
01:27:16.511 --> 01:27:20.038
She had marks on her nose.
I'm giving you that.

1360
01:27:20.114 --> 01:27:22.207
From glasses, right?

1361
01:27:22.283 --> 01:27:25.616
She didn't wear them out of the house
so people'd think she's gorgeous.

1362
01:27:26.687 --> 01:27:28.712
But when she saw this kid
killing his father...

1363
01:27:28.789 --> 01:27:31.019
she was in the house, alone.

1364
01:27:31.092 --> 01:27:32.957
That's all.

1365
01:27:33.494 --> 01:27:37.658
Do you wear glasses to bed?
- No, I don't.

1366
01:27:39.200 --> 01:27:41.134
No one wears eyeglasses
to bed.

1367
01:27:41.202 --> 01:27:43.932
It's logical to assume she wasn't
wearing them when she was in bed...

1368
01:27:44.005 --> 01:27:45.734
tossing and turning,
trying to fall asleep.

1369
01:27:45.806 --> 01:27:48.206
How do you know?
- I don't know. I'm guessing.

1370
01:27:48.276 --> 01:27:51.370
I'm also guessing she didn't
put her glasses on when she turned...

1371
01:27:51.445 --> 01:27:52.969
to look casually
out of the window.

1372
01:27:53.047 --> 01:27:56.141
And she herself testified the killing
took place just as she looked out.

1373
01:27:56.217 --> 01:27:58.811
Lights off a split second later. She
couldn't have had time to put them on.

1374
01:27:58.886 --> 01:28:00.854
Wait a second.
- Here's another guess.

1375
01:28:00.922 --> 01:28:03.254
Maybe she honestly thought
she saw the boy kill his father.

1376
01:28:03.324 --> 01:28:04.951
I say she only saw a blur.

1377
01:28:05.026 --> 01:28:08.689
How do you know what she saw?
How does he know all that?

1378
01:28:08.763 --> 01:28:11.527
How do you know what kind of glasses
she wore? Maybe they were sunglasses.

1379
01:28:11.599 --> 01:28:13.999
Maybe she was farsighted.
What do you know about it?

1380
01:28:14.602 --> 01:28:17.833
I only know the woman's eyesight
is in question now.

1381
01:28:17.905 --> 01:28:23.537
She had to be able to identify a person
60 feet away at night without glasses.

1382
01:28:23.611 --> 01:28:26.011
You can't send someone off to die
on evidence like that.

1383
01:28:26.614 --> 01:28:28.878
Don't give me that.

1384
01:28:28.950 --> 01:28:31.316
Don't you think the woman
might have made a mistake?

1385
01:28:31.385 --> 01:28:34.548
It's not possible?
- No, it's not possible.

1386
01:28:37.858 --> 01:28:39.792
Is it possible?

1387
01:28:40.895 --> 01:28:42.487
Not guilty.

1388
01:28:46.701 --> 01:28:49.329
Do you think he's guilty?

1389
01:28:49.403 --> 01:28:51.337
I think he's guilty.

1390
01:28:54.141 --> 01:28:55.733
Do you?

1391
01:29:00.147 --> 01:29:03.241
I'm convinced. Not guilty.

1392
01:29:03.317 --> 01:29:07.151
What's the matter with you?
- I have a reasonable doubt now.

1393
01:29:07.221 --> 01:29:08.654
Eleven to one.

1394
01:29:08.723 --> 01:29:12.022
What about all the other evidence?
What about all that stuff?

1395
01:29:12.093 --> 01:29:14.084
The knife? The whole business?

1396
01:29:14.161 --> 01:29:16.652
You said we could
throw out all the other evidence.

1397
01:29:22.803 --> 01:29:25.237
Well, what do we do now?

1398
01:29:28.142 --> 01:29:32.340
You're alone.
- I don't care whether I'm alone or not.

1399
01:29:32.413 --> 01:29:34.313
It's my right.

1400
01:29:36.817 --> 01:29:38.648
It's your right.

1401
01:29:48.029 --> 01:29:50.190
What do you want?
I say he's guilty.

1402
01:29:52.133 --> 01:29:56.092
We want to hear your arguments.
- I gave you my arguments.

1403
01:29:56.704 --> 01:30:00.265
We're not convinced.
We want to hear them again.

1404
01:30:01.575 --> 01:30:04.203
We have as much time
as it takes.

1405
01:30:09.116 --> 01:30:13.849
Everything, every single thing
that took place in that courtroom...

1406
01:30:13.921 --> 01:30:15.684
but I mean everything...
says he's guilty.

1407
01:30:15.756 --> 01:30:18.190
What do you think,
I'm an idiot or something?

1408
01:30:19.827 --> 01:30:21.954
Why don't you take that stuff
about the old man...

1409
01:30:22.029 --> 01:30:25.192
the old man who lived there
and heard everything.

1410
01:30:26.867 --> 01:30:30.598
Or this business about the knife.
What, 'cause they found another like it?

1411
01:30:30.671 --> 01:30:34.107
The old man saw him
right there on the stairs!

1412
01:30:34.175 --> 01:30:36.575
What's the difference
how many seconds it was?

1413
01:30:38.145 --> 01:30:40.079
Every single thing.

1414
01:30:41.515 --> 01:30:44.814
The knife falling through a hole
in his pocket.

1415
01:30:44.885 --> 01:30:48.184
You can't prove
he didn't get to the door!

1416
01:30:48.255 --> 01:30:50.689
Sure, you can take all the time,
hobble around the room.

1417
01:30:50.758 --> 01:30:53.056
But you can't prove it!

1418
01:30:55.730 --> 01:30:58.995
And what about this business
of the El and the movies?

1419
01:30:59.066 --> 01:31:02.058
There's a phony deal
if I ever heard one.

1420
01:31:02.136 --> 01:31:06.266
I'll bet you $5,000
I'd remember the movies I saw.

1421
01:31:06.340 --> 01:31:08.570
I'm telling you.
Everything that's gone on...

1422
01:31:08.642 --> 01:31:12.476
has been twisted and turned.

1423
01:31:12.546 --> 01:31:16.175
This business with the glasses? How
do you know she didn't have them on?

1424
01:31:16.250 --> 01:31:19.276
This woman testified
in open court!

1425
01:31:20.521 --> 01:31:24.457
And what about
hearing the kid yell, huh?

1426
01:31:24.525 --> 01:31:27.494
I'm telling you,
I've got all the facts here.

1427
01:31:28.863 --> 01:31:30.490
Here.

1428
01:31:33.100 --> 01:31:35.830
That's it.
That's the whole case.

1429
01:31:42.810 --> 01:31:44.243
Well?

1430
01:31:46.747 --> 01:31:49.443
Say something!

1431
01:31:53.854 --> 01:31:58.052
You lousy bunch
of bleeding hearts.

1432
01:31:58.125 --> 01:32:02.118
You're not going to intimidate me.
I'm entitled to my opinion.

1433
01:32:05.766 --> 01:32:09.930
Rotten kids.
You work your life out!

1434
01:32:27.288 --> 01:32:28.880
Not guilty.

1435
01:32:50.044 --> 01:32:51.978
We're ready now.

1436
01:34:51.198 --> 01:34:54.361
What's your name?
- Davis.

1437
01:34:54.435 --> 01:34:56.369
My name's McCardle.

1438
01:34:58.806 --> 01:35:01.907
Well, so long.
- So long.

